\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The Workers Win in Alabama\b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions and their political allies claim the glory days of union organizing are back, especially in the South. One problem: workers. So it went last week in Alabama, where employees at two \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  plants rejected the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in a vote that wasn't close, 56% to 44%.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, the tough-talking \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, accused Mercedes of intimidating workers and engaging in illegal behavior, which is the classic excuse after a union loses an election. "This loss stings," Mr. Fain said. "There are more than 2,000 workers at Mercedes in Alabama who want to join our union. They aren't going away." But neither are the 2,600 Mercedes workers in Alabama who didn't want the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's no blaming poor turnout or worker apathy, since about 4,700 ballots were cast, out of 5,100 eligible employees. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  could and no doubt will try again, as it did with the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Chattanooga, Tenn., where the union succeeded in April on its third try in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Still, it's apparent that many workers aren't interested in what the union is selling, which is less about job security than progressive priorities that extend well past the workplace. Mr. Fain presents himself as the vanguard of left-wing politics, as when he denounced Israel's effort to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  "has been calling for a ceasefire for six months," he said recently. Why should line workers in Tuscaloosa pay dues for that?\par \par \uc2 Unions keep losing labor-market share, and official figures show the downward slide hasn't stopped. Last year 10% of workers were union members, down from 10.1% in 2022. In the private economy, the rate was unchanged at 6%. But among public workers it fell to 32.5%, from 33.1%. Such annual changes might look small, but that's how erosion works, a little at a time. In 2000 the private workforce was 9% unionized, and for public employees it was 36.9%.\par \par \uc2 This trend is why union leaders increasingly rely on their political allies to tilt the negotiating rules to make it easier for unions to organize while limiting how employers can fight back. It's why unions dislike right-to-work laws in the South that let individual workers have the choice of whether to join a union.\par \par \uc2 But when employers are able and willing to make the case against putting Mr. Fain in charge of their workplaces, workers who like their jobs will listen.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Workers Vote Down Unions. Will Biden's \uc2 NLRB\uc2  Listen?" -- WSJ May 28, 2024)\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Funny How \uc2 UAW\uc2  Never Loses Fair and Square" -- WSJ June 3, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240520ek5k00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
