\par\par \b \uc2 Employers Face More Scrutiny Over Teen Sex Abuse\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Weber \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A kitchen worker repeatedly groped a teenage employee at the Palo Alto location of Il Fornaio, a chain of Italian restaurants in California, and later raped her off-site.\par \par \uc2 A manager at a Shane's Rib Shack restaurant in Georgia spread false sexual rumors about an 18-year-old worker and made daily comments about her appearance. When she complained, her supervisor said she invited the incidents by the way she dressed and later fired her without explanation.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  restaurant in West Virginia hired a supervisor who had previously been convicted of sexually assaulting two minors and allowed him to supervise young female employees, two of whom said he sexually harassed them.\par \par \uc2 These are among dozens of allegations in complaints filed in recent months by attorneys and by the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , which has increased its scrutiny of what it said is a persistent problem: sexual harassment of teens in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 About a quarter of young workers experience sexual harassment on the job, according to a 2023 study, with some of them as young as 14 years old.\par \par \uc2 Employers are relying more on young workers to fill jobs amid a tight labor market. In April this year, 5.8 million 16- to 19-year-olds were working, the highest April count since 2009. That number surges in the summer.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EEOC\uc2  made the protection of teenage workers in low-wage jobs a priority in a four-year strategic plan released in September. In the past two years, it has sued or reached settlements with small operators as well as big chains -- including \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill\uc2  and a large \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchisee -- where young, mostly female workers said they have been harassed.\par \par \uc2 Companies often settle these suits without admitting wrongdoing, while agreeing to a monetary settlement and changes to how they handle complaints.\par \par \uc2 "For a lot of teens, this is their first work experience," \uc2 EEOC\uc2  Commissioner Kalpana Kotagal said in an interview. "To be exposed to harassment can have ripple effects on someone's career. It can affect their confidence and their sense of self-worth in the workplace."\par \par \uc2 Young workers often don't report problems such as harassment, according to a study examining violence against 14- to 24-year-old workers. The study, based on a 2018 survey of more than 1,000 young workers, was published last year by researchers at \uc2 Boise State University\uc2 , the \uc2 University of Iowa\uc2  and the \uc2 University of California San Diego\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Seventy-five percent of the young people who experienced sexual harassment told someone about what happened, with most of those confiding in a friend or co-worker, according to preliminary data from the survey. A little over half reported it to a supervisor and 33% told a parent.\par \par \uc2 "It takes a tremendous amount of courage for any survivor of sexual harassment to come forward, much less a minor," said Todd Bailess, whose firm is representing the two West Virginia Taco Bell employees in lawsuits against Charter Foods, a \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  franchisee owned by Nascar team owner Bob Jenkins. \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  and its parent, \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2 , weren't named in the suits, one of which has been settled.\par \par \uc2 A representative from Charter Foods declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Young workers often "don't know they have a right to say, 'This isn't OK,'" said Kimberly Rauscher, a professor of public health at \uc2 Boise State University\uc2  and one of the authors of the study. Some teens believe that sexual harassment is simply an odious but inevitable part of the job, Rauscher said.\par \par \uc2 Most receive no training on how to deal with unwanted comments or touching, she said. And teenagers are often eager to impress their bosses, stay out of trouble and prove they can handle the responsibilities that come with a job. Some also need the money and are afraid they will be fired if they report problems.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for alleged victims said workers who report harassment or assault often encounter unreceptive bosses. Companies might have the right policies in the employee handbook, but many don't take steps to prevent or stop harassment any harassment, Bailess said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's current enforcement push shows employers they will face consequences if they don't have systems in place to investigate complaints and protect workers, said Chris DeGroff, an employment attorney at \uc2 Seyfarth Shaw\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In September, Chipotle agreed to pay $400,000 after the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  sued it for failing to investigate or stop a manager and a co-worker from harassing three young employees in Washington state. The manager allegedly sexually assaulted one of the young women, who was 17 at the time. Chipotle didn't admit wrongdoing and said in a written statement that it is providing additional training in some restaurants.\par \par \uc2 "We're really calling this out," \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's Kotagal said. "It's really important that employers have multiple avenues for reporting, that antiharassment policies are real and known about, that they're training managers about company policies, and that when faced with complaints, they're adequately and appropriately investigating and taking remedial steps."\par \par \uc2 At Il Fornaio, sexual harassment was constant, severe and pervasive across multiple locations, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  said in a complaint filed last month, and the perpetrators included company executives and restaurant managers as well as hourly employees. Complaints about harassment, even when escalated to Il Fornaio's headquarters, were routinely ignored, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  alleged.\par \par \uc2 Among dozens of allegations, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  said the general manager at one location groped a teenage employee and repeatedly asked underage employees, all girls, to go out and get drunk with him. At another location, according to the complaint, a kitchen worker told a 17-year-old hostess, "With a face like that, who wouldn't want to go to jail?"\par \par \uc2 Il Fornaio denies that the company fostered or tolerated a culture of sexual harassment. The company "takes harassment seriously and disputes the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's claim that it allowed harassment at its restaurants," its attorney said in a written statement, adding that the chain has increased its prevention efforts. In legal filings, Il Fornaio said the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's lawsuit exceeded the scope of its investigation.\par \par \uc2 In the suit involving Shane's Rib Shack in Georgia, the franchisee agreed to pay $56,500 to settle the case. Lawyers for the franchisee didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich309\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4635
