\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Businesses, Government See Some Progress in Cyber Hiring\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Rundle \par \uc2 640 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SAN FRANCISCO -- Federal and private-sector initiatives are generally making improvements to boost the U.S. cyber workforce, but some areas, including diversity, require urgent attention, officials and experts say.\par \par \uc2 There are enough workers to fill 82% of the open cybersecurity positions in the U.S., translating to just over 448,000 jobs across the country, according to data from the Commerce Department and trade associations. In 2023, the national average was 72%, or around 572,000 open jobs.\par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt in my mind that there is enough talent in the U.S. to fill those jobs, and then some," National Cyber Director Harry Coker Jr. said during a panel at the RSA Conference here last week. The key, he said, is enticing people into the field.\par \par \uc2 "We need cyber workers in each and every sector," said Seeyew Mo, assistant national cyber director for cyber workforce, training and education, in an interview on the sidelines of the conference.\par \par \uc2 ISC2, a professional and certification group in cybersecurity, launched an entry-level certificate in September 2022 to introduce people with no background in cyber to the industry. Around 54,000 people have since been certified through the program, said Clar Rosso, ISC2's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 Globally, ISC2 estimates that around four million cybersecurity professionals are needed to meet global security needs. While the uptake for entry-level certifications is encouraging, Rosso said, problems are starting to emerge.\par \par \uc2 In Asia-Pacific, for example, workers who gain expertise in cybersecurity often migrate to regional hubs such as Australia, leaving their home countries with a deficit in cyber workers. Additionally, although ISC2 is seeing good improvement with nonwhite men moving into security roles after getting certified, the same can't be said broadly across underrepresented groups.\par \par \uc2 "We're making no progress whatsoever with women," Rosso said, adding that ISC2 has convened a task force to figure out how to better support individuals with seeking work.\par \par \uc2 "I would just say more to come on that space, because we're not making progress on the gender issue at all," Rosso said.\par \par \uc2 Workforce issues were a prominent element of the Biden administration's national cybersecurity strategy, published in March 2023. In July, the White House issued a national workforce strategy, which secured commitments from various agencies and private-sector companies to establish apprenticeship and recruitment programs and training curricula.\par \par \uc2 Around 7,000 cyber apprentices have registered, ONCD's Mo said. Similar programs in the government and private sectors aim to get people who don't have two- or four-year college degrees into the industry.\par \par \uc2 The ONCD is working on a local level to stimulate cybersecurity training, from K-12 schools to community colleges. The White House is trying to work with local partners to establish programs that make sense for a region, he said.\par \par \uc2 For instance, if a large manufacturing plant or utility is a significant employer in a particular area, the ONCD will work with schools to provide training that meets the specific needs of both that industry and cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 "The workforce in San Antonio is very different from the workforce in San Francisco, so you need people to be the anchor and drive those changes," he said. The ONCD is planning to expand its visits to local areas in the coming year.\par \par \uc2 Mo said that commitments from critical infrastructure operators, such as railroad operator Union Pacific, to cross-train employees in cyber, is evidence that interest in cyber workforce issues is reaching beyond just technology companies.\par \par \uc2 The private sector in recent years has launched programs designed to increase hiring into cybersecurity, such as accessible training courses, partnerships with colleges, and programs to attract candidates with nontraditional educational or work backgrounds who may nevertheless be suited to a career in cyber.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240513ek5d0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
