\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Illinois Pension Suicide Pact\b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Constitution isn't a suicide pact, but the same can't be said for the way government unions run Illinois. Behold the new union strategy to kill the state's pension reforms using Social Security as the political cudgel.\par \par \uc2 Following state credit downgrades, Illinois Democrats in 2010 reduced pension benefits for new state and local government workers. Current workers weren't affected. The modest reforms raised the retirement age to 67 from 60, capped the final earnings that are used to calculate annual pension benefits, and reduced retiree annual cost-of-living adjustments.\par \par \uc2 These so-called Tier 2 modifications are saving the state and its localities billions of dollars a year. Yet unions now claim they violate a 1990 federal law that requires government worker pensions to be at least as generous as Social Security benefits. Otherwise, public employers and employees must pay the 12.4% Social Security payroll tax.\par \par \uc2 Government pension and Social Security benefits are calculated using completely different formulas, which makes direct comparisons difficult. But the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  has adopted an arcane and arbitrary formula to determine whether government pensions in aggregate are "comparable" to benefits they'd collect if they paid into Social Security.\par \par \uc2 This gets technical, so bear with us. Illinois's Tier 2 pension reform capped the maximum wage base for calculating benefits of new employees at $106,800 -- the same as the Social Security wage base in 2010. Tier 2 also limited the wage base's annual growth to one-half of the increase in the consumer price index or 3%, whichever is less.\par \par \uc2 The purpose was to reduce the state's growth in liabilities over time by limiting benefits that new high-earning employees accrue. But because of rapid inflation, the maximum wage for Illinois's pension calculation is now much lower than for Social Security -- $125,774 versus $168,600. Unions claim this disparity causes Tier 2 to run afoul of federal law under the \uc2 IRS\uc2  formula.\par \par \uc2 This is nonsense. The maximum Social Security benefit this year for a 70-year-old is $58,476. A Tier 2 worker can retire with a roughly $94,000 annual annuity. Illinois Tier 2 pensions are much more generous than Social Security. In any case, most Illinois workers earn less than the state's $125,774 pension wage cap and wouldn't be affected by it.\par \par \uc2 If high inflation persists, some high-earning Tier 2 workers might decades from now retire with a smaller benefit than they would get from Social Security. In that case Illinois could top off these workers' pensions to ensure they are equal to what they would receive from Social Security.\par \par \uc2 The unions are after much more. They are lobbying state lawmakers to restore for newer workers the more generous pension benefits that employees receive if they were hired before 2011. This would cause Illinois's already gargantuan pension liabilities to swell even more. Its pensions are the nation's worst-funded. About 22% of state general fund tax revenue goes to paying for pensions each year.\par \par \uc2 The unions' dubious interpretation of federal law would also effectively give the \uc2 IRS\uc2  a veto over state pension reforms. As Social Security benefits grow with inflation and squeeze the federal fisc, state pension benefits would have to grow at the same rate or faster. Thus, the suicide pact.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  could lobby the Treasury Department to revise the \uc2 IRS\uc2  formula to head off a union lawsuit. But he'd have to take on the unions and look out for taxpayers, which he has never done. If unions succeed in rolling back Illinois's pension modifications, they are certain to try the same in other states.\par \par \uc2 President Biden could order Treasury to help save Illinois taxpayers from rapacious unions, but you can guess how that will go. And when the pensions run out of money, the unions will head to Washington for a bailout.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240507ek570000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
