\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Unemployment Rate In IT Edges Down\b0\par\par\uc2 By Belle Lin \par \uc2 560 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for U.S. information-technology workers ticked down to 2.8% in April from 3% in March, according to trade group \uc2 CompTIA\uc2 , a sign some companies might be hiring to support their artificial intelligence-related projects.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies, such as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , added about 4,280 workers in April, according to \uc2 CompTIA\uc2 's analysis, which is based on Labor Department data. The growth was led by hiring for technology services and software development positions, followed by cloud infrastructure, data processing and data hosting jobs, the group said.\par \par \uc2 Data and cloud underpin the use of generative AI inside companies -- providing the foundation to run the AI models and link them with private data so businesses can gather insights on their own information -- even if it isn't used right now.\par \par \uc2 "This could be an investment towards future growth, not necessarily the fact that every single company right now is pouring resources into AI," said \uc2 Tim Herbert\uc2 , \uc2 CompTIA\uc2 's chief research officer. "That's probably driving a fair amount of the cloud growth that we're seeing."\par \par \uc2 Technology services such as IT consulting have also been on the rise in 10 of the past 12 months, part of companies' efforts to bring in outside help to assist with AI initiatives, Herbert added.\par \par \uc2 Across the economy, however, job growth slowed. U.S. employers added a seasonally adjusted 175,000 jobs in April, the Labor Department said Friday. That is a vastly different picture than in March, when job gains surpassed 300,000. The overall unemployment rate rose to 3.9% in April from March's 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 Still, technology chiefs across sectors are cautious of bringing on workers while they sort out how AI will impact various IT roles.\par \par \uc2 Simon Green, chief information officer of cloud-software distributor \uc2 Pax8\uc2 , said some tech leaders are starting to view AI as an alternative to hiring.\par \par \uc2 The thinking is, "slow your hiring down and invest in licensing around AI instead, because you can look at it as a force multiplier," he said.\par \par \uc2 There is growing evidence that AI is starting to affect entry-level roles in IT, a trend that took root when companies began applying automation to reduce manual work, said \uc2 Victor Janulaitis\uc2 , chief executive of consulting firm Janco Associates.\par \par \uc2 "Companies are continuing to look at how to reduce costs," Janulaitis said, and that is pressuring leaders to hire more selectively for specific skills. "The general statistics are that the employment picture for IT professionals has not improved all that much."\par \par \uc2 CompTIA\uc2 's analysis separates jobs at technology firms, which can include workers in areas like sales and marketing, from IT jobs at companies outside the tech sector, such as retailers, banks or hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Tech jobs throughout the economy fell by 0.3%, or 20,000 jobs, in April, the group said. Employers listed fewer new jobs last month, adding 179,000 job postings, a drop from the 191,000 postings added in March.\par \par \uc2 Mixed job indicators -- such as the unemployment rate improving despite fewer jobs -- aren't unusual when the broader economic outlook is uncertain, Herbert said. "There are lots of nervous CIOs and C-suite leaders. They're probably a little bit reluctant to hire, not knowing what's going to happen next quarter, next six months," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240506ek560000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
