\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Locks Out Firefighters At Factory --- Airplane maker and union have been wrestling over wages, staff levels\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  locked out about 130 firefighters who work at its 737 factory and other operations in Washington state following a contract dispute between the jet maker and the firefighters' union.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union have been wrestling for months over wages and staffing levels for firefighters who handle medical emergencies and accidents at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  facilities.\par \par \uc2 The firefighters cover factories that make 737, 767 and 777 jets as well as \uc2 Boeing\uc2  facilities in Seattle and Moses Lake. They also are on hand for all test and delivery flights or whenever a jet is fueled.\par \par \uc2 The company said it secured its own firefighters to replace the unionized crew and has a contingency plan in place to ensure operations and production won't be affected by the lockout.\par \par \uc2 Since a Jan. 5 midair blowout, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has been dogged by quality issues that have slowed production and drawn increased regulatory scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 It is also in separate contract talks with unions representing thousands of mechanics and other employees in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  warned the firefighters union that it would lock workers out at 12:01 a.m. Saturday without a deal. The union's members twice voted down proposed deals, and negotiations earlier in the week failed to bring the sides closer together.\par \par \uc2 "We have now locked out members of the bargaining unit and fully implemented our contingency plan," the company said in a statement shortly after the deadline passed. "Our plan ensures safe operations."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Association of Fire Fighters\uc2  argues that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  pays its members wages well below those of surrounding municipal departments and that it takes too long -- 19 years, under the company's proposal -- to reach maximum pay. It says the force is short staffed.\par \par \uc2 "Our people love \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , they love their jobs," said Casey Yeager, president of the \uc2 IAFF\uc2  local representing the firefighters. "But we can't retain people, because the pay is so low."\par \par \uc2 The firefighters last year responded to an average of 225 calls a month. They receive specialized training to deal with chemicals and fuels involved in airplane manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  says pay levels and the time it takes to reach top wages should match that of other corporate firefighting forces, not that of municipal departments. It says staffing levels are higher than those that apply to corporate forces.\par \par \uc2 The company says the average firefighter made $91,000 a year, which it says is competitive. The union says that pay starts at $52,000 and tops out at about $85,000, and that \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's figure includes significant overtime.\par \par \uc2 "The parties are not close to reaching an agreement," Michael Gitto, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's labor-relations director, wrote in a letter threatening Yeager with a lockout. "The company has gone as far financially as it is willing to go and will not add any more money to its offer."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240506ek560001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
