\par\par \b \uc2 Fast-Food Prices Get Upsized in California\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 961 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Restaurants for months have said menu prices in California would rise as the state raised the minimum wage for fast-food workers. Now they are following through.\par \par \uc2 Consumers picking up burgers, burritos and chicken sandwiches at chains in the Golden State are grappling with prices that for months have been rising at a faster clip than in other states, according to market-research firm Datassential.\par \par \uc2 Since September, when California moved to require large fast-food chains to bump up their minimum hourly pay to $20 in April, fast-food and fast-casual restaurants in California have increased prices by 10% overall, outpacing all other states, the firm found in an analysis of thousands of restaurants across 70 large chains.\par \par \uc2 Prices at \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 , Domino's, \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , Pizza Hut, \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2  and other fast-food chains have increased since September, the firm found. Chipotle said in an investor call Wednesday that prices at its nearly 500 California restaurants climbed 6% to 7% during the first week of April compared with last year, playing out across its menu.\par \par \uc2 "The state isn't making it easy," Chipotle Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles on a recent April afternoon, Seth Amitin, a 39-year-old therapist, said his usual $16 meal that he picks up weekly at the \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  in Hollywood now costs $20. The price for a spicy chicken sandwich at that location had gone up to $7.09 from $6.29, or 13%, since mid-February, according to research by Gordon Haskett Research Advisors. \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 's prices increased 10.6% on average in California during that time period, \uc2 Gordon Haskett\uc2  found.\par \par \uc2 "There's a really good taco spot just down the street. They kept their burrito prices at $10. I'm definitely eating there more often," Amitin said.\par \par \uc2 California raised the minimum wage for fast-food workers to $20 an hour in April, a 25% increase from the state's broader $16 minimum wage. Supporters, including California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , a Democrat, said the law would improve wages and working conditions for hundreds of thousands of fast-food employees in the state.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the governor said fast-food companies can afford to give their workers a deserved bump in pay. "These are wages that will go towards basic necessities like rent and groceries," he said.\par \par \uc2 The restaurant industry spent millions of dollars fighting the wage law and was ready to mount a referendum campaign but then negotiated a deal with backers last September. \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2 , \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  and others said they would raise menu prices in California in response, with some \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchisees estimating hundreds of thousands of dollars per restaurant in added labor costs.\par \par \uc2 California restaurants already had some of the highest fast-food prices in the country, according to market-research firm \uc2 Revenue Management Solutions\uc2 . Every month since October, California fast-food and fast-casual restaurants have raised prices across a greater percentage of their menus compared with restaurants in the rest of the country, Datassential found.\par \par \uc2 Greg LaVay, a 79-year-old retired entrepreneur from San Diego who worked in the retail office furniture business, said he noticed \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's hamburgers in his area creeping up to $2.50 each. A Big Mac now goes for $5.39, he said. He said he used to visit \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's a few times a month but a few months ago started to limit his restaurant spending to sit-down restaurants for dinner.\par \par \uc2 "I feel ripped off a little," he said.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant owners working for franchised chains such as \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  and \uc2 Burger King\uc2  set their own prices, while Chipotle and other companies that own their domestic stores determine their menu costs.\par \par \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's said it is committed to providing good value for customers, and the company and its franchisees set up a team to aid its California business. "\uc2 McDonald\uc2 's is competitive when it comes to affordability across the state," the company said.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2  spokeswoman said the company has increased prices around 7% in its company-owned stores compared with last year. She said the company and its franchisees have sought to run stores more efficiently in response to the increased costs associated with the wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Customers across the country are starting to pull back on restaurant visits after eateries pushed up their prices in response to inflation, industry data show.\par \par \uc2 California consumers like Chandu Siram, a 50-year-old architect in Eastvale, said he previously didn't bat an eye at how much his family of four spent on fast food but now has started to make fewer runs to Chipotle and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . He might go further.\par \par \uc2 "For now, we are keeping track of our spending at these places and if our spending goes up, we'll look to cut back," Siram said. He is heading more often to \uc2 Popeyes Louisiana Kitchen\uc2  for deals at the chicken chain.\par \par \uc2 A number of California consumers said they were shifting their restaurant spending to independent eateries, considering them a better value and experience than some chains. The California wage law doesn't apply to restaurants with fewer than 60 national locations.\par \par \uc2 John Matthews, a 62-year-old project manager from Imperial Beach, Calif., said he believes the fast-food wage law is a drag on the state economy, and he doesn't support it. He said he has shifted his roughly $600 in monthly restaurant spending to independent, sit-down restaurants and away from \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, Chipotle and other chains.\par \par \uc2 "I'm still eating out, though much more selectively," Matthews said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Talal Ansari contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich452\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6780
