\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Presses Justices to Limit \uc2 NLRB\uc2  --- High court case on union drives comes as other companies air their own disputes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erin Mulvaney \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  asked the Supreme Court on Tuesday to limit a government labor board that has been eager to support union drives, a case that comes as other top companies mount aggressive defenses against the agency.\par \par \uc2 In the Biden era, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has pursued a bold enforcement agenda to bolster worker protections. The board, which referees disputes between employers and workers, has expanded pathways for forming a union and made it easier for employees to challenge workplace practices. It also has broadened the types of remedial compensation workers can receive if an employer treats them unfairly.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has brought a dozen cases seeking injunctions against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  alone, challenging the chain's responses to unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 With an aggressive board, union organizing has increased across the U.S. In 2023, more than 100,000 workers organized in \uc2 NLRB\uc2 -conducted elections, the largest in a single year since 2000.\par \par \uc2 The organizing push has expanded into the technology sector, retail and other areas.\par \par \uc2 "The amount of union activity is surging where you would not expect to see it," said Steven Suflas, who practices employment law at \uc2 Holland & Hart\uc2 . "And some of the companies suing have acrimonious relationships with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  these days."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  case at the Supreme Court is an outgrowth of a unionizing campaign in Memphis, Tenn. The union alleged the company engaged in unlawful tactics, culminating with the firing of seven union activists. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  filed a complaint alleging unfair labor practices and asked a federal court to issue a temporary injunction. A judge ordered the interim reinstatement of the terminated employees, among other things. An appeals court affirmed that order.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said the fired employees broke several company policies, including by allowing a news crew into the store after hours to promote the unionization drive. The company petitioned the Supreme Court to hear the case on the grounds that some lower courts -- including the judges in this litigation -- have followed a legal standard that makes it too easy for the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to win injunctions.\par \par \uc2 William Gould, a former \uc2 NLRB\uc2  chair and law professor at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 , said the specific legal question before the court-whether a two-part or four-part legal test should be used -- may not have a huge impact, but he said the justices' interest in the case has broader significance.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that they took this case indicates they are listening to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , and they think the board should somehow be reined in," he said.\par \par \uc2 During Tuesday's oral arguments, several conservative justices questioned whether the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 -friendly standard used by some courts is legally sound. Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  asked why the board should be treated better than other government litigants seeking injunctions.\par \par \uc2 Voicing more support for the board, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  said the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's cases were different than other litigation, and that legal rules should take into account the board's role in policing unfair labor practices.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  recently agreed to resume collective-bargaining talks with the union this week. Workers at hundreds of stores have voted to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Some companies are making arguments against the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in litigation, including that the board's entire structure is unconstitutional. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , which faces a board complaint for allegedly firing workers who criticized \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , is pressing this claim in a federal lawsuit against the board, a five-member body that currently has three Democrats and one Republican, with one seat vacant. Most initial labor complaints and election petitions are handled by regional administrative law judges. The company said this structure violates its right to a jury trial and that there should be more executive oversight of board members.\par \par \uc2 In defending labor-board complaints against them, \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , Trader Joe's and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are also making this argument.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has said those claims are meritless. It also points to a 1937 Supreme Court decision that upheld the constitutionality of the board.\par \par \uc2 In other recent litigation, the board has had its share of both wins and losses. It beat \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in a case in March over its stopping workers from distributing union buttons. It lost against \uc2 Tesla\uc2  over its barring employees from wearing union T-shirts instead of company-issued uniforms.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's policies and enforcement can vary considerably between administrations. The pendulum swing has been particularly pronounced since President Biden's nominees replaced the leadership team put in place by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are in a critical moment," \uc2 NLRB\uc2  General Counsel \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2  said in an interview, citing workers' interest in organizing. "You're seeing deep-pocketed, low-road employers who are breaking the law, and then trying to prevent us from enforcing the statute. And unfortunately, they are doing it because they have money."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240424ek4o0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
