\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Google\uc2  Fires 28 Employees For Protesting Israeli Deal --- Company says its offices were defaced and work was impeded by sit-in\b0\par\par\uc2 By Miles Kruppa \par \uc2 678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's \uc2 Google\uc2  said it fired 28 employees who protested this week against the tech giant's cloud-computing contract with the Israeli government, the latest example of how debates about the war in Gaza are rippling across some workplaces.\par \par \uc2 In a companywide email, the vice president for global security, Chris Rackow, said \uc2 Google\uc2  dismissed the employees after an investigation found they were involved in the protests at the company's offices in New York and Sunnyvale, Calif.\par \par \uc2 Rakow said in the Wednesday email that the fired employees "took over office spaces, defaced our property and physically impeded the work of other Googlers," violating company policies.\par \par \uc2 That crossed a line for \uc2 Google\uc2 , which historically has encouraged debate among employees.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  on Thursday said that while preserving \uc2 Google\uc2 's open culture is important, the company also needs to enforce workplace policies and be "more focused in how we work, collaborate, discuss and even disagree."\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  wasn't a place "to fight over disruptive issues or debate politics," Pichai wrote in an email announcing a restructuring of the company's devices and mobile software divisions. "This is too important a moment as a company for us to be distracted," he said.\par \par \uc2 No Tech for Apartheid, the group that organized the protests, said in a statement that it attributed to \uc2 Google\uc2  workers involved in the campaign: "This flagrant act of retaliation is a clear indication that \uc2 Google\uc2  values its $1.2 billion contract with the genocidal Israeli government and military more than its own workers -- the ones who create real value for executives and shareholders."\par \par \uc2 The group disputed that all of the 28 employees who were fired directly participated in the protests and said those involved didn't damage property or threaten others. \uc2 Google\uc2  said it confirmed that all 28 had participated in the protests using video footage.\par \par \uc2 Israel has denounced claims that it is committing genocide against Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 The episode illustrates how tech companies, long hotbeds of debate, are trying to limit internal unrest and get employees to focus on work while emphasizing business efficiency. \uc2 Google\uc2  employees have a history of protesting military-related contracts and have won some victories in the past.\par \par \uc2 The latest protests followed an article in Time magazine that said \uc2 Google\uc2  provides cloud services for the \uc2 Israeli Ministry of Defense\uc2 , citing company documents. \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in 2021 signed a contract, known as Nimbus, providing cloud services to the Israeli government.\par \par \uc2 Police in California and New York arrested nine \uc2 Google\uc2  employees Tuesday following sit-in protests that lasted 10 hours, according to No Tech for Apartheid.\par \par \uc2 Protesters in Sunnyvale occupied an office used by Google Cloud CEO \uc2 Thomas Kurian\uc2 . At both sites, protesters said they planned to occupy \uc2 Google\uc2  office spaces until they were either arrested or \uc2 Google\uc2  canceled its contract with Israel. Employees in New York sat down in a common space and unfurled a banner reading, "No Tech for Genocide."\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Google\uc2  said earlier this week that the contract wasn't aimed at being used for weapons or intelligence work, some employees are worried \uc2 Google\uc2  is still aiding Israel's war efforts. Protesters have argued \uc2 Google\uc2  has little control over how Israel uses its technology.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has a history of open internal debate around workplace and political issues. At a companywide meeting following Donald Trump's 2016 election as president, \uc2 Google\uc2  executives, including co-founder \uc2 Sergey Brin\uc2 , lamented his victory and debated the impact the company's services had on the election.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 Google\uc2  decided not to renew a contract with the Pentagon code-named Project Maven after it became the subject of intense internal debate.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees have also protested the contract with Israel. Amazon Employees for Climate Justice, a group that has taken issue with the company's environmental policies, in February called on the company to cancel the Nimbus contract.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment on Nimbus.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240419ek4j0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
