\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Justices Back Workers on Discrimination --- Job transfers can violate civil-rights law even if pay or rank stay the same\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Wolfe and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 590 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court made it easier Wednesday for workers to bring claims challenging job transfers as discriminatory, ruling that allegations of illegal treatment can be valid even if an employee's pay or rank don't change.\par \par \uc2 The court revived a lawsuit by a female police sergeant in St. Louis, who said she was unlawfully transferred from the intelligence division to a less prestigious patrol position because she is a woman.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court in St. Louis dismissed the suit, concluding that Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 requires a plaintiff to show she suffered significant harm from a discriminatory act.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the court, Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  said that while an employee must show more than trifling harm from an employer's discriminatory action, the appeals court had raised the bar too high.\par \par \uc2 "'Discriminate against' means treat worse, here based on sex. But neither that phrase nor any other says anything about how much worse," Kagan wrote. "There is nothing in the provision to distinguish, as the courts below did, between transfers causing significant disadvantages and transfers causing not-so-significant ones."\par \par \uc2 Kagan's decision was joined by Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Three other justices agreed with the outcome in separate opinions. One, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , said even the majority's test was too stringent for employees. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , in contrast, wrote that Kagan failed to give lower courts sufficient guidance in applying the law.\par \par \uc2 The decision is the latest in a string of high-court rulings that have bolstered worker protections under Title VII, which prohibits employment discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex or national origin.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiff in Wednesday's case, Jatonya Clayborn Muldrow, worked as a plainclothes sergeant with the St. Louis Police Department, managing cases about public corruption and human trafficking. The role came with several perks, she said, including challenging work and an \uc2 FBI\uc2 -issued vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Muldrow got a new supervisor in 2017, who transferred her and replaced her with a man. According to Muldrow's suit, her new position involved mundane administrative and patrol tasks, without the perks she enjoyed previously. She was never formally demoted.\par \par \uc2 Her lawyer, Brian Wolfman, said Wednesday's decision reiterates that Title VII "means what it says: you can't discriminate in the workplace."\par \par \uc2 The city's lawyers said Muldrow was reassigned, along with many other male and female officers, as part of a routine staffing reallocation. Bob Loeb, who argued for St. Louis at the high court, said the city looked forward to showing that Muldrow can't meet the new standard.\par \par \uc2 The decision likely means more charges of discrimination will flow to the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , said \uc2 Janet Dhillon\uc2 , who served as \uc2 EEOC\uc2  chair during the last two years of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers and workers have long filed discrimination charges regarding actions such as job transfers, undesirable shift assignments and reductions in duties or titles, but the agency was limited by courts' interpretation of what constituted potential Title VII violations. Now, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  will investigate those with a more expansive view, Dhillon said.\par \par \uc2 Workers still face hurdles getting discrimination claims before juries, said \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2  law professor Suja Thomas. "Unfortunately, there are lots of other ways courts are dismissing these cases," Thomas said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240418ek4i0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
