\par\par \b \uc2 Resurgent \uc2 UAW\uc2  Sets Its Sights On VW Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Colias \par \uc2 844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A historic effort by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  to unionize more than a dozen factories outside of Detroit faces its first test this week, as workers at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's lone U.S. plant cast ballots.\par \par \uc2 More than 4,000 employees at VW's sport-utility-vehicle factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., were scheduled to begin voting Wednesday on whether to be represented by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , with results expected by the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 The 89-year-old \uc2 UAW\uc2  has yet to unionize any foreign-owned auto plants in the U.S., many of which are in Southern states where antiunion sentiment runs deep and right-to-work laws serve as a deterrent.\par \par \uc2 A rejuvenated \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership is seeking to harness momentum from historic contracts it won late last year at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The union got pay increases of 25% over more than four years, enhanced retirement benefits and other gains, after a six-week strike that included walkouts at nine U.S. factories. The vote in Chattanooga is shaping up to be a referendum on whether \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain's worker-empowerment message will resonate with workers outside the union's industrial-Midwest stronghold.\par \par \uc2 Fain wants to leverage the union's success in Detroit to expand its reach and membership, which has gradually declined from 1.5 million members in the 1970s. As of 2023, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  had roughly 370,000 members -- with many of them collected in other sectors, such as gambling and higher education. \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders say conditions are ripe to snap the union's losing streak among auto workers in the South.\par \par \uc2 "When we return to the bargaining table in 2028, it won't just be with the Big Three, but with the Big Five or Big Six," Fain said late last year.\par \par \uc2 Workers behind the unionization effort at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  said they want to join the \uc2 UAW\uc2  for more pay, better work schedules and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 VW said it has "some of the best-paying jobs in the area" and is proud of its working environment at the plant.\par \par \uc2 "We fully support an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  vote so every team member has a chance to a secret ballot vote on this important decision," the company said, referring to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VW's European factory workforce is unionized, and labor officials hold several seats on the German automaker's supervisory board.\par \par \uc2 For decades, Detroit executives complained the \uc2 UAW\uc2  contracts leave them with heavier labor costs than \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 , \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  and other foreign rivals with nonunion U.S. workforces. In 2022, foreign automakers paid a top wage of about $28 an hour, compared with about $32 at the three Detroit companies.\par \par \uc2 The new \uc2 UAW\uc2  contracts secured in the fall further boosted the top pay for factory workers at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to roughly $42 an hour. Since then, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and several foreign automakers doled out significant wage increases to their U.S. workers.\par \par \uc2 Beyond VW, the union wants to organize factories of about a dozen other automakers, including \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2  and electric-vehicle maker \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , accounting for about 150,000 workers. That is roughly the same number covered under the contracts with \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , at factories mostly in union-friendly states in the Great Lakes region.\par \par \uc2 A unionization vote at VW's Chattanooga factory failed in 2014, and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  fell short again in a close vote in 2019. Workers at a \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  plant in Mississippi in 2017 also rejected \uc2 UAW\uc2  representation.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, a group of six Southern governors, including Tennessee Gov. Bill Lee, issued a letter in opposition to the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's organizing drive, claiming it would threaten their auto-industry investments and the health of the companies.\par \par \uc2 "Unionization would certainly put our states' jobs in jeopardy," reads the letter, also signed by the governors of Alabama, Georgia, Mississippi, South Carolina and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Fain's team seized on a confluence of macro factors that aided the union's historic contract wins in Detroit last fall: a tight labor market, high inflation and a record run of carmaker profits -- all set amid a galvanized U.S. labor movement.\par \par \uc2 In the past few years, other labor unions mounted successful organizing drives at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores, an \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  warehouse and the Dartmouth men's basketball team. And unions notched several collective-bargaining wins -- at \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , Deere and other companies.\par \par \uc2 Fain was elected \uc2 UAW\uc2 's president in March 2023 on a message of change, after years of corruption in its top ranks.\par \par \uc2 A unionization vote by about 5,000 \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  workers at the German automaker's plant near Tuscaloosa, Ala., is expected next month after enough signatures were gathered to call for a vote under \uc2 NLRB\uc2  rules, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has said.\par \par \uc2 In Chattanooga, workers will vote up or down on the question of whether they want to be represented by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  in collective bargaining. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is overseeing the vote and expects to have results by late Friday.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich468\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7020
