\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Salt Lake City and Florida Have the Hottest Job Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Angel Au-Yeung \par \uc2 817 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Follow the mountains and sunshine for the best job markets in America.\par \par \uc2 Salt Lake City was the country's hottest job market in 2023, followed by three cities in Florida: Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa. Miami also made the top 10, making Florida the top state last year.\par \par \uc2 The Mountain West and Sunbelt cities bucked the trend in a year marked by layoffs in the technology, manufacturing and financial sectors. Workers flocked to these areas for their plentiful job opportunities, wage growth, affordability and recreational offerings.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal, working with \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics, assessed around 380 metro areas. The rankings determined the strongest labor markets based on five factors: the unemployment rate, the labor-force participation rate, changes to employment levels, the size of the labor force and wages. Larger areas, with more than a million residents, were ranked separately from smaller ones.\par \par \uc2 Data continue to show a strong U.S. economy, tempering expectations for interest-rate cuts by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . Employment in March rose by 303,000 jobs, according to the Labor Department, which was much higher than economists' expectations, while the unemployment rate ticked down to 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 Cities with strong job markets were those well-positioned as affordable alternatives to the traditional tech and financial hubs.\par \par \uc2 For over a decade, Salt Lake City has attracted white-collar workers from the San Francisco Bay Area and Los Angeles. Many of the workers have taken jobs in Salt Lake City's tech hub, aptly referred to "Silicon Slopes." They have also been able to buy homes after being priced out of California cities. This migration only increased after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "There's a virtuous cycle where young, highly educated workers are moving into Salt Lake City and bringing in more money to that area," said \uc2 Adam Kamins\uc2 , an economist at \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics.\par \par \uc2 Salt Lake City performed well across all five metrics, with the second-highest rate of labor-force participation and fifth-strongest growth in wages.\par \par \uc2 Austin, Texas, which fell five spots from the previous year but was still the seventh-highest-ranked labor market, had the strongest labor-force participation among large metro areas. Like Salt Lake City, Austin has become a cheaper alternative to the traditional tech hubs.\par \par \uc2 While high interest rates and layoffs also affected Salt Lake City last year, it was easy for those workers to find employment given the area's diverse economy, said Ron Zarbock, owner of Spherion Staffing Services, which specializes in professional, information technology and logistics industries.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the IT startups got diminished, but other organizations from the financial markets or manufacturing would pick them right up," said Zarbock.\par \par \uc2 Friendly business laws and tax incentives have also made Salt Lake City a good place to build and expand businesses in other industries, said Jill Perelson, founder and chief executive of recruiting agency PrincePerelson & Associates. High-growth industries in Salt Lake City include life sciences, manufacturing and supply-chain logistics.\par \par \uc2 "There is so much land here that manufacturing and logistics companies can expand into the desert and beyond," said Perelson.\par \par \uc2 In January, food company Mars announced a new 399,000 square-foot baking facility to make Nature's Bakery snacks. Subzero Engineering, maker of data-center equipment, announced a 155,000-square-foot manufacturing facility in December.\par \par \uc2 Florida dominated the top 10 rankings of the hottest job markets in 2023 with four spots, after holding three of the top 10 spots in 2022. Jacksonville has continued moving up the ranks, becoming the second-hottest job market after placing third in 2022.\par \par \uc2 It is due to a continuation of companies ranging from corporate offices to warehouses relocating from the Northeastern corridor to Jacksonville, said Mike Brady, owner of two \uc2 Express Employment Professionals\uc2  staffing offices. His firm specializes in recruiting warehouse employees in the area. The abundance of jobs related to the gig economy has also forced employers to offer more competitive wages, he said.\par \par \uc2 Florida as a whole continues to be a magnet for remote workers who like the affordability and the lack of a state income tax, said Kamins at \uc2 Moody's\uc2 . Banks, mortgage lenders and real-estate businesses hired aggressively last year in Florida.\par \par \uc2 While those industries didn't have a banner year in 2023, Florida outperformed the rest of the U.S., Kamins said.\par \par \uc2 Hedge-fund giant \uc2 Citadel\uc2  announced two years ago that it was moving its headquarters to Miami. Financial firms including Fifth Third and PGIM said in recent months they would open offices in Tampa.\par \par \uc2 Tampa and Miami came in first and second, respectively, in wage growth -- indicating that employers are paying up to attract and retain workers. Meanwhile, New York and San Jose, Calif., metro areas, which are major financial and tech hubs, ranked toward the bottom of the list for wage growth.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw270\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4050\pichgoal9600
