\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Union Pushes Back Against U.S. Steel Deal --- To win over workers, \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  pitches new investment in some aging plants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 1,159 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's Mon Valley Works mill in recent years has endured a debilitating fire and millions of dollars in regulatory fines. The steelmaker abandoned plans for a $1.2 billion upgrade, and analysts have speculated that the mill could eventually close.\par \par \uc2 Now, the collection of aging plants along the Monongahela River southeast of Pittsburgh is taking on a central role in \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's $14.1 billion gambit to acquire 123-year-old \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Union workers who staff \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's last production sites in the region of what was once Steel City have been stewing over the company's 2021 shelving of the planned upgrade, which would have been the biggest investment in Mon Valley in decades. Stung by \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's decision to expand production at a nonunion plant in Arkansas, the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  union has accused executives of turning their backs on plants that have served the company for more than a century.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the union, which represents about 10,000 hourly workers at \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , now oppose \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's planned takeover. After years of plant closures and workforce reductions, the union and its allies in Congress have warned that the deal could lead to further underinvestment in plants and potentially undermine U.S. national security.\par \par \uc2 To change their minds, \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has told union leaders that it would consider reviving \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's plans for Mon Valley as part of an offer to the union to invest $1.4 billion in upgrading \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's older mills. \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  executives have pledged to refrain from layoffs and plant closings through the end of the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 ' current contract in 2026.\par \par \uc2 "Prove it," said Don Furko, president of the union local for \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's coking coal plant for Mon Valley. "They would have to give us some details about what they plan on doing." \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  leaders said they want a formal commitment on investment and other changes.\par \par \uc2 Opposition formed quickly after \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  and \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  announced their deal in December, and hasn't budged. Democratic and Republican lawmakers have called for the deal to be stopped. President Biden signaled his opposition to foreign ownership of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  last month, though he didn't say explicitly that he would block the deal, now under national-security review by the \uc2 Committee on Foreign Investment in the U.S.\uc2  last month, though he didn't say explicitly that he would block the deal, now under national-security review by the\par \par \uc2 That review is expected to take months. Opponents cite \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's business in China as potential grounds for blocking the sale. The committee can recommend that the president block a deal, but it usually prescribes remedies. Consultants and lawyers said it would be unprecedented for a president to overrule \uc2 Cfius\uc2  if its review finds no security risks.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  said its business in China accounts for less than 5% of its global production capacity. "The entities in which we invest in China have no control over our operations or business decisions outside of China," the company said.\par \par \uc2 Behind the scenes, a rival steel company, \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  -- which itself tried to acquire \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  last year with a lower bid -- has been urging lawmakers to fight the deal. \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Lourenco Goncalves\uc2  continues to proclaim his interest in taking another run at \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  if the \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  deal collapses.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  shareholders are scheduled to vote on the sale April 12.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  union endorsed Biden's re-election on March 20, about a week after the president said \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  should remain domestically owned.\par \par \uc2 Winning over the steelworkers union could help \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  neutralize many of the political obstacles to a deal. "The minute \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  and the steelworkers reach an agreement, all the opposition comes off the boil and goes away," said Elena McGovern, managing director for Washington-based \uc2 Capstone\uc2 , a national-security and business-policy consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 That partly hinges on convincing the union that \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  will be a different company under \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In December, Executive Vice President \uc2 Takahiro Mori\uc2  said the Japanese company found a "life partner" in \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  and expected to largely continue its business strategies. To \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's union critics, that suggested little change to the frayed relationship with the company. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  in recent years has closed large mills near Detroit and St. Louis, shrinking the union workforce by about 4,000.\par \par \uc2 Contract negotiations in 2022 between \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  and the union dragged on for months before an agreement was reached. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  agreed in the contract to invest $1 billion in unionized plants, less than half the amount in the previous contract.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  has had a checkered environmental record, particularly at Mon Valley, where a 2018 fire damaged the Clairton coke plant, knocking out emissions-control equipment for months. The episode drew safety complaints from the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  and millions of dollars in environmental fines.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  said in 2021 its need to invest in steelmaking with lower carbon-dioxide emissions and difficulty in obtaining local permits caused it to shelve the $1.2 billion upgrade of the Mon Valley Works, which would have reduced the production time for sheet steel with a new caster and a new rolling line.\par \par \uc2 "We know that this difficult decision is the right one for the business," CEO \uc2 David Burritt\uc2  told analysts in April 2021. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  redirected money and equipment for the Mon Valley upgrade to a new, nonunion mill in Arkansas. As that mill's production capacity has expanded, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  has closed older mills.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  said it would honor the terms of the 2022 labor contract. The \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  union doesn't have the right to simply reject \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  as a buyer, but the contract contains a successorship clause requiring the company to maintain benefits, pensions, profit-sharing and other provisions specified in the existing labor agreement.\par \par \uc2 The union filed a grievance against \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , saying the company didn't keep the union informed about acquisition offers. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  said it complied with all requirements in the labor agreement and called the \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  deal the best path forward for employees.\par \par \uc2 The union on April 2 said it rejected a draft of a binding commitment to the labor agreement offered by \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 , because the company included conditions or exceptions that the union said could be used to avoid commitments.\par \par \uc2 "For every commitment that the Nippon parties purport to make, the proposal envisions a way to release Nippon from these pledges," \uc2 United Steelworkers International\uc2  President \uc2 Dave McCall\uc2  wrote in a letter to Mori.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240408ek480001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
