\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 A California 'Right to Disconnect'?\b0\par\par\uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Progressives in Sacramento are becoming increasingly disconnected from the real economy. The latest evidence? A new California bill that would establish an employee "right to disconnect" and ignore a boss's emails after normal working hours.\par \par \uc2 The Assembly bill is modeled after laws in a dozen or so countries, including such paragons of productivity as Argentina, France, Greece, Italy and Spain. "Smartphones have blurred the boundaries between work and home life," says the bill's sponsor \uc2 Matt Haney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some workers may bristle at receiving emails beyond the normal eight-hour workday. But this is the flip side of more flexible work arrangements and smartphones. Workers have more freedom to pick up children from school, but they are sometimes expected to respond to important emails while at their kids' soccer games.\par \par \uc2 The Assembly bill would grant employees a legal right to ignore an employer's communications during "nonworking hours" except for emergencies or work scheduling. All employment contracts in the state would also have to clearly delineate working and nonworking hours. California's Labor Commissioner could fine employers who violate workers' "right to disconnect."\par \par \uc2 If an employer or a client experiences a problem on a weekend, tough. Managers couldn't ask workers to help unless the problem is an "unforeseen situation that threatens an employee, customer, or the public; disrupts or shuts down operations; or causes physical or environmental damage." The Labor Commissioner and courts would presumably define what is "unforeseen." Would a news event qualify?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Haney says "workers shouldn't be punished for not being available 24/7 if they're not being paid for 24 hours of work." But hourly workers are already required to be paid overtime if they clock more than eight hours a day. Salaried employees, on the other hand, are often paid more to be on-call and work longer hours. Some bosses no doubt exploit employees in their off-hours, but then those bosses tend to lose the best workers.\par \par \uc2 Progressives say workers with a right to disconnect are healthier, happier, and more productive. If so, employers have an incentive to limit after-hour communications and work. Yet American workers are more productive than their counterparts in other countries in part because U.S. employment laws and arrangements are less rigid. Progressives already want to dictate a four-day workweek.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Haney hilariously claims the legislation will make California businesses "more competitive for desperately needed workers" and "the California tech sector better able to compete for skilled workers" with other states. Has he been to Silicon Valley, where 9 to 5 working hours are far from the norm for tech bros?\par \par \uc2 California's employment has fallen by 82,600 over the last year. It has lost jobs in most private industries, including information (-54,200), professional and business services (-33,400), trade, transportation and utilities (-19,600), finance (-13,200) and manufacturing (-12,500). A major culprit is burdensome business regulation driving away employers.\par \par \uc2 Progressive ideas that originate in Sacramento have a habit of becoming mainstream in the Democratic Party, which is why we have to cover them. Too bad there's no way for the rest of the country to disconnect from California's unreal politics.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240406ek460001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
