\par\par \b \uc2 Backlash Against Israel Mounts After Deadly Strike on Aid Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Kalin \par \uc2 1,072 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Israel struggled to contain a growing crisis after a strike by its military killed seven aid workers in Gaza, drawing condemnation from around the globe and putting more pressure on the country to lower the civilian toll of its campaign to wipe out the militant group \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The deadly strike late Monday that hit workers with \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  -- an aid group founded by celebrity chef Jose Andres and one of the most important providers of food assistance in the Gaza Strip -- risks further isolating Israel and adds to the friction with the U.S., its chief ally.\par \par \uc2 Concerns had been increasing internationally over the course of the war, which has killed around 33,000 people in Gaza, mostly women and children, according to Palestinian health authorities, whose numbers don't distinguish between militants and civilians.\par \par \uc2 But the deaths -- including the first known among foreign aid workers in Gaza since the start of the war in October -- galvanized pressure on Israel to protect people caught up in the fighting.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said he was "outraged and heartbroken" by the aid workers' deaths, in a statement released Tuesday night by the White House. He said an Israeli probe into the deaths must be done quickly and made public.\par \par \uc2 "Incidents like yesterday's simply should not happen," he said. "Israel has also not done enough to protect civilians. The United States has repeatedly urged Israel to deconflict their military operations against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  with humanitarian operations, in order to avoid civilian casualties."\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  of Israel acknowledged that his country was responsible for the strike. "Unfortunately, in the past day there was a tragic incident of an unintentional hit by our forces on innocent people in the Gaza Strip. It happens during war," he said in a recorded message on X. "We are in touch with governments and we'll do everything so that this doesn't happen again."\par \par \uc2 Defense Minister Yoav Gallant launched an inquiry into the killings and said the military would set up direct coordination between the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  and international aid organizations.\par \par \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  identified those killed in the attack as Saifeddin Issam Ayad Abutaha, 25 years old, from the Palestinian territories; Lalzawmi "Zomi" Frankcom, 43, of Australia; Damian Sobol, 35, of Poland; Jacob Flickinger, 33, of the U.S. and Canada; and John Chapman, 57, James "Jim" Henderson, 33, and James Kirby, 47, all from the UK.\par \par \uc2 Monday's incident unfolded after a convoy of ships organized by \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  that departed from Cyprus over the weekend had begun to unload some 400 tons of food in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The aid organization said its workers -- who were traveling in two armored cars branded with the group's logo, and a third vehicle -- were hit as they left a warehouse in the central Gaza city of Deir al-Balah, where they had deposited food from the ships. One of the vehicles had a hole torn through its roof; another appeared entirely burned out.\par \par \uc2 The Washington-based charity said its staff had coordinated their movements ahead of time with the Israeli military, which it said had conducted a "targeted attack."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  spokeswoman said the ships were sent back to Cyprus after the incident, having unloaded a quarter of the food they were meant to deliver. The group said it had paused its work across the Middle East, which includes operations in Lebanon, Egypt and Cyprus.\par \par \uc2 The rules of war require that combatants avoid targeting civilians and humanitarian groups that provide aid to the population. In complex conflict zones like the Gaza Strip, the United Nations and other aid groups voluntarily share the locations of their offices, warehouses and other premises with the warring parties to carve out safe zones and avoid getting hit by mistake.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, they also coordinate on their movements, such as on aid-delivery missions, to avoid being caught in fighting. In Gaza, this is standard practice by all humanitarian groups. Missions to northern Gaza, deemed especially high-risk, require approval from Cogat, the Israeli military body coordinating aid in the territory. It denies most requests.\par \par \uc2 Even so, humanitarian buildings and convoys have been hit multiple times in Gaza. It isn't clear why the so-called deconfliction mechanism has repeatedly failed to keep aid workers safe. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  has largely halted aid missions to the north since early February, after Israeli navy fire struck an aid convoy while it waited to cross a checkpoint inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military said its forces were targeting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  infrastructure and that it would learn lessons from the incident.\par \par \uc2 "This is not an isolated incident," said Jamie McGoldrick, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  humanitarian coordinator for the occupied Palestinian territory, referring to Monday's strike. Since October, at least 196 humanitarian workers have been killed in the Palestinian territories, he said, making them one of the most dangerous places in the world to work.\par \par \uc2 "I am heartbroken and grieving for their families and friends and our whole \uc2 WCK\uc2  family," Andres said on X. "The Israeli government needs to stop this indiscriminate killing. It needs to stop restricting humanitarian aid, stop killing civilians and aid workers, and stop using food as a weapon. No more innocent lives lost."\par \par \uc2 Israel denies using food as a weapon and said it is constantly trying to ease the flow of aid into Gaza. The amount of aid that has been entering Gaza since Israel launched its war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  there has been vastly inferior to the basic needs of the enclave's 2.2 million residents, humanitarian groups said.\par \par \uc2 More than one million people are estimated to be starving in Gaza as a result of the war Israel launched against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in response to the Oct. 7 attacks, which Israeli authorities say killed 1,200 people, mostly civilians.\par \par \uc2 Competing political agendas and conflicting war aims have pushed the relationship between Netanyahu and Biden toward rupture. Biden has pressed for more humanitarian aid into Gaza, withheld support for a planned Israeli operation in the southern city of Rafah and left open the possibility of withholding some U.S. weapons deliveries unless Israel takes further steps to protect civilians.\par \par \uc2 He has made clear, however, that the U.S. would never abandon Israel.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 David Winning, Drew Hinshaw, Margherita Stancati, Carrie Keller-Lynn and Annie Linskey contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240403ek430001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
