\par\par \b \uc2 Cava Won't Lift California Prices Despite Labor Costs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Williams \par \uc2 496 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At least one restaurant chain plans to hold the line on what it charges customers even as others are raising prices in California because of the state's minimum-wage boost for fast-food workers.\par \par \uc2 Mediterranean-style restaurant chain Cava says its recent growth will allow it to absorb the higher labor costs without lifting menu prices. And with a smaller footprint in California than other chains that have said they would raise prices, \uc2 Cava\uc2  may not feel as much pain from higher labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Keeping prices where they are for menu items like falafel and harissa honey chicken is the right move for consumers who are increasingly fatigued by higher fast-food prices, the company's finance chief said.\par \par \uc2 Cava\uc2  expects an impact of 0.3 percentage point to overall margins because of the change, according to Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Tricia Tolivar\uc2 . "But being thoughtful and mindful around the consumer in California, we're not planning to put that on their back," she added. \uc2 Cava\uc2 's recent growth and increased foot traffic means the company can keep prices where they are. "We're going to absorb that in the business overall and evaluate it ongoing," Tolivar said.\par \par \uc2 Cava\uc2  had raised menu prices nationwide around 3% in January in an effort to offset ingredient inflation.\par \par \uc2 Cava\uc2 's labor costs will increase this year because of the law setting minimum wage for California fast-food workers at $20 an hour in April, a 25% increase from the state's broader $16 minimum wage. Restaurant chains from \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill\uc2  to \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2  and \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  are planning to lift prices in the state in response to the change.\par \par \uc2 Analysts estimate the law in California, where roughly 7% of Cava's 309 restaurants are located, will mean raising Cava's wages in the state by around 17%. In 2022, California passed a law putting oversight of the fast-food industry in the hands of state-appointed councils that could have raised pay to as much as $22 an hour. The restaurant industry allocated tens of millions of dollars to invalidating the law before a compromise was reached that set the new $20 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Total labor costs are already among the top line items for many restaurants, typically about 30% of sales with hourly labor making up most of that, said Brian Vaccaro, a senior restaurant analyst at \uc2 Raymond James & Associates\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For those increasing prices, the higher checks will come as the cost of eating out has already been running high. Prices for food eaten away from home were up 5.1% in January compared with a year earlier and up 30% from the same month in 2019, Labor Department data showed.\par \par \uc2 The Washington-based company's revenue for its namesake chain surged by more than 52% compared with a year earlier to $175.5 million for the quarter ended Dec. 31.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
