\par\par \b \uc2 The New Workplace (A Special Report) --- For Lower-Income Workers, Recent Economic Gains Endure: Hiring and wage growth have slowed amid an economic soft landing, but probably won't backslide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe \par \uc2 933 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For a while, the pandemic looked like a turning point for the lowest-paid workers in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 As the economy reopened, workers who lack college degrees and often work in-person found themselves in high demand. Their wages benefited disproportionately from historically tight labor markets, even as corporate cutbacks targeted white-collar professionals. One study hailed a reversal of the decadeslong trend of widening wage inequality.\par \par \uc2 Now, three years on, the situation is turning out to be more nuanced. Some of that boom may have been temporary; hiring and wage growth is cooling for many blue-collar occupations. ("Blue collar" and "low income" are often used interchangeably, even though some blue-collar jobs pay quite well.) And while white-collar layoff announcements continue, the overall layoff rate is low.\par \par \uc2 Economists say that while low-income workers' fortunes aren't as likely to outperform as much as in recent years, they aren't about to start backsliding again, thanks to a solid economy, retirements, a premium for in-person work and less exposure to the job-displacing effects of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The result could be slower, more-uniform job growth across different industries, economists says. Economic growth, job creation and consumer spending are expected to retreat this year from strong levels in 2023, but economists don't foresee a recession.\par \par \uc2 "We're getting back to equilibrium," says Brad Hershbein, senior economist at the Upjohn Institute for Employment Research. Job growth has "been slowing in a lot of sectors, especially in sectors that had been overheated, things like tech," he says, adding that hiring has also cooled in some blue-collar industries, such as leisure and hospitality. That sector -- which includes restaurants, hotels and bars -- had some of the deepest cuts in the initial months of the pandemic, then the fastest jobs growth as the economy emerged from lockdowns. Hiring has ebbed as the industry approaches prepandemic employment levels.\par \par \uc2 Pay raises for leisure and hospitality workers far outpaced that for workers overall for several months in late 2021 and early 2022. But wage growth slowed across the economy during 2023, collapsing the premium these workers were receiving. As of January's jobs report, average hourly earnings were up 4.4% from a year earlier for leisure and hospitality workers, compared with 4.5% for private workers overall.\par \par \uc2 Some blue-collar industries are seeing softness. Transportation and warehousing employers have shed more than 99,000 jobs as of January's jobs report since peaking in July 2022. That was a far cry from the hot hiring streak the industry had during an e-commerce boom in the wake of the pandemic, which cooled off as Americans resumed spending on services.\par \par \uc2 But going in the opposite direction, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's likely cuts to interest rates should help interest-sensitive industries with lots of blue-collar jobs such as manufacturing and construction, says Kory Kantenga, senior economist at \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Construction employment reached new highs last year, amid a building boom and as federal investments in infrastructure began to flow. Hiring in manufacturing was essentially flat during 2023 as the industry slumped.\par \par \uc2 What about for office-based, college-educated jobs (often grouped together as "white collar")? The consulting, finance and technology sectors have been hit with announcements of large-scale layoffs, as companies look to streamline operations, cut costs or downsize after aggressive hiring early in the pandemic. \uc2 Google\uc2  parent company \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , \uc2 eBay\uc2 , \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  are among the high-profile technology companies that have announced job cuts.\par \par \uc2 Still, Labor Department data show the number of monthly layoffs in the information industry, which includes software and data-processing companies, mostly declined over 2023. Kantenga says that aligns with \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  figures that show churn in the sector broadly is declining.\par \par \uc2 "That adjustment has started to come down," he says. "They've sort of gotten to the point where they have recalibrated."\par \par \uc2 So what does the future hold? In a study first published last year and since updated, Arin Dube of the \uc2 University of Massachusetts-Amherst\uc2  and co-authors found that, between 2019 and 2023, wages adjusted for inflation had risen most for the lowest 20% of paid workers. The only quintile to see its wages decline was the top 20%. A lot of the gains at the bottom came from workers changing to better paying, higher-quality work, the authors found.\par \par \uc2 Dube says he thinks prospects for lower-income workers are still favorable over the long term.\par \par \uc2 "Going forward," he said in an email, "I think the combination of demographic change (retirement of baby boomers) as well as the possibility that AI will substitute for (as opposed to complement) skilled workers, can all point to more favorable conditions for lower-income workers. The usual caveats about uncertainty in these early days of AI should be kept in mind."\par \par \uc2 To be sure, if the economy slips into recession instead of a soft landing, lower-earning Americans could be hit harder, says \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP. She says such workers are less likely to receive severance pay in the case of a layoff, for example.\par \par \uc2 "Lower-income workers bear the brunt of choppiness and volatility and they are the most vulnerable," Richardson says.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Anthony DeBarros and Greg Ip contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Amara Omeokwe is a writer in the Washington, D.C., area. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240311ek3b00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
