\par\par \b \uc2 Cooling Labor Boosts Rate-Cut Hopes --- Hiring far exceeds expectations, but joblessness ticks up, wage growth slows\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Uberti \par \uc2 863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Steady hiring and cooling wage growth last month offered the latest evidence that the U.S. economy is making progress toward a so-called soft landing that brings inflation down without a recession.\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers added 275,000 jobs in February, according to the Labor Department, far exceeding the 198,000 that economists had expected.\par \par \uc2 But behind the headline number were signs of a gradual slowdown. The unemployment rate ticked up to 3.9%, higher than expected, and wage growth slowed. Gangbusters data from January were revised sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 The Goldilocks report lends credence to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's outlook that somewhat lower interest rates could be warranted later this year, potentially providing a boost to markets that have been on a tear to start 2024.\par \par \uc2 Bill Adams\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Comerica Bank\uc2 , summed up Friday's report with one word: cool. "That's what the Fed wants to see right now," he said.\par \par \uc2 Stocks rose following the report but retreated in afternoon trading. The S&P 500 ended 0.7% lower, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell by 0.2%, and the Nasdaq Composite Index declined 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields held recent declines, another sign that investors believe rate cuts could be on tap in coming months.\par \par \uc2 The market's early bullishness was driven in part by the Labor Department's substantial revisions to previous employment data. Officials said a month ago that the economy had added 353,000 positions in January. On Friday, they said the number was actually 229,000.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings in February rose just 0.1% from the previous month, short of Wall Street's expected 0.2%. That was also a significant slowing from a revised 0.5% in January.\par \par \uc2 A month ago, those red-hot January numbers drew warnings from economists and investors that price pressures were rebounding. But Friday's mixed report, which smoothed out weather-related abnormalities in that earlier data, allayed those fears.\par \par \uc2 Investors in recent weeks have grown increasingly confident in the U.S. economy, which has muscled through the highest interest rates in 20-plus years with consistent job growth and some of the lowest unemployment rates in a half-century.\par \par \uc2 President Biden trumpeted those gains in his State of the Union address on Thursday, pointing to investments spanning renewable energy and manufacturing. "The landing is and will be soft," he said.\par \par \uc2 The key question that will ripple from the U.S. economy into financial markets is one of timing.\par \par \uc2 Should the Fed keep rates too high for too long, it could stifle economic growth and risk a recession. But cutting too early could help reignite inflation by lowering borrowing costs for businesses making capital improvements, as well as consumers buying new homes or cars.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  previously poured cold water on the idea of a rate cut at the central bank's March 19-20 meeting. Investors assess the chances of a rate cut in June as better than 50%, according to interest-rate futures.\par \par \uc2 In hearings on Capitol Hill this past week, Powell told lawmakers that the Fed is seeking greater certainty that inflation is sliding closer to the central bank's 2% target before it begins easing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "When we do get that confidence -- and we're not far from it -- it will be appropriate to dial back," he said. The \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  on Thursday also left its key interest rate unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, the U.S. economy has added an average of 230,000 jobs a month. A growing immigrant workforce appears to have helped.\par \par \uc2 The number of native-born Americans with a job fell by 881,000 over the 12 months through February, to 129.3 million, according to the Labor Department. But the number of foreign-born workers with a job rose by 1.5 million, to 31 million.\par \par \uc2 Employers across healthcare, government and food services led the labor market's gains in February. Labor-force participation held steady for the third consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 Americans' wage growth since last year continues to run higher than what would be consistent with the central bank's target. But the labor market is broadly normalizing with "sturdy gains that are not overly exuberant," said \uc2 Kathy Bostjancic\uc2 , chief economist for Nationwide Mutual.\par \par \uc2 "It's an overall solid picture for the economy," she added.\par \par \uc2 Friday's report comes after widely watched indicators in recent weeks suggested that the final stages in Washington's fight against price pressures might prove tricky.\par \par \uc2 Still, those data did little to slow financial markets' steady climb higher, with every sector of the S&P 500 posting gains over the past month. The recent rally has suggested the tech-fueled optimism of last year is broadening to include a wider swath of the U.S. economy, said Thorne Perkin, president of the investment firm Papamarkou Wellner Perkin.\par \par \uc2 "This is exactly what the market was looking for," he said of Friday's report. "Stocks still have more room to run."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Nick Timiraos and Paul Kiernan contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
