\par\par \b \uc2 Railroad Unions Back CEO Against Activist\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union workers in recent years have threatened to strike against \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  and sparred with management over pay and safety practices. Now, workers are defending the freight railroad as an activist investor seeks to oust its leadership.\par \par \uc2 Labor groups representing Norfolk conductors, locomotive engineers, machinists and other workers have made public comments in support of Chief Executive \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2  as he comes under pressure from activist Ancora Holdings. The groups account for over half of the railroad's unionized workforce.\par \par \uc2 Unions don't typically take sides in corporate proxy battles -- it is the shareholders who vote for directors and investor proposals. But union leaders said they are speaking up because their members have seen an improvement in safety and morale under Shaw and worry that a management shake-up will reverse these gains.\par \par \uc2 Ancora\uc2  said it launched a proxy fight to overhaul Norfolk's board and C-suite because the railroad has inefficient operations and failed to meet financial targets. It also said Norfolk should undertake scheduled railroading with more rigor, and that it mismanaged the aftermath of last year's Ohio train derailment that released toxic chemicals into the surrounding water and land, triggering a massive cleanup effort.\par \par \uc2 Ancora\uc2  reiterated its call for new leadership after three Norfolk trains were involved in a derailment Saturday in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Norfolk has defended its efforts to improve the company, especially after the February 2023 derailment. \uc2 Ancora\uc2 's proposed overhaul would be "highly disruptive to our operations, our workers, and the North American supply chain," the company said.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Ancora\uc2 's plan will decimate the railroad all over again," said Scott Bunten, a Roanoke, Va.-based union official and locomotive engineer who has worked for Norfolk for 19 years. The \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2  plans to vote its hundreds of thousands of shares in Norfolk in support of the company's board nominees, a union official said.\par \par \uc2 Ancora\uc2  has proposed replacing Shaw with \uc2 Jim Barber\uc2 , former chief operating officer at \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 . It also would replace operations chief Paul Duncan with \uc2 Jamie Boychuk\uc2 , who served in a similar role at rival \uc2 CSX\uc2 . Shaw took the helm at the Atlanta-based railroad in May 2022.\par \par \uc2 "We talk about cost-cutting, we talk about the opportunity to grow the business, to get the right economics, on the right network strategy," said Barber in an interview. "If and when the growth comes back the right way, then jobs should grow."\par \par \uc2 Other shareholders have raised concerns about Norfolk's performance. Among major railroads, its shares declined by the largest percentage last year and some operating metrics show that it has been less efficient than its peers. Norfolk's shares, which traded below $200 last year, have climbed above $250 since The Wall Street Journal reported earlier this year that an activist was proposing changes.\par \par \uc2 Norfolk and the Cleveland-based hedge fund are drumming up support for their respective board nominees ahead of the company's annual meeting, which typically is in May.\par \par \uc2 A potential return to precision scheduled railroading, or PSR, has rankled union workers and regulators, who also have criticized \uc2 Ancora\uc2 's approach.\par \par \uc2 It was around 2017 when major freight railroads started implementing PSR as a strategy to boost profits by running trains more efficiently and reducing costs.\par \par \uc2 Activist investors pressured the railroads to adopt PSR, which boosted shareholder returns, but rail service suffered and crews complained about worsening working conditions and increased fatigue. Norfolk's employment declined 32% between the end of 2018, when it introduced PSR, and 2021.\par \par \uc2 "It's the same old corporate strategy that has been hanging around and we've been pushing back for years," said Martin Oberman, chairman of the \uc2 Surface Transportation Board\uc2 , the economic regulator of major freight railroads. "For \uc2 Ancora\uc2  to condemn the management, it shows a lack of understanding of moving freight around the country."\par \par \uc2 Barber said he recognizes that there is a stigma to PSR, and he welcomes the opportunity to sit down with union leaders and explain the plan. \uc2 Ancora\uc2  isn't emphasizing cost cutting or head-count reductions in its presentations to investors, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Ancora\uc2  has pushed for executive and board changes at other freight companies, including \uc2 C.H. Robinson Worldwide\uc2  and \uc2 Forward Air\uc2 . Last year, \uc2 Ancora\uc2  pushed for Barber to be chief executive at C.H. Robinson, but that role went to someone else.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the \uc2 Federal Railroad Administration\uc2  and \uc2 STB\uc2  have warned of increased scrutiny of Norfolk if safety incidents and shipping delays increase after potential operating changes. Regulators were deluged with complaints from shippers about delays and trip cancellations as a result of thin staffing levels during the Covid-19 pandemic. The railroads have since stepped up hiring efforts and boosted staffing levels from their pandemic lows.\par \par \uc2 Train crews at Norfolk said they appreciate having more colleagues in the past year as it has helped with scheduling and proper rest days. They also said there is a less antagonistic working environment with managers, which helps with retention.\par \par \uc2 "In the past, it was more, 'you did this, you're in trouble,'" said Matt Tyler, a licensed conductor and locomotive engineer based in Altoona, Pa., adding there is now a more collaborative approach.\par \par \uc2 This time last year, another activist investor targeted \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 , looking to replace the chief executive with \uc2 Jim Vena\uc2 , a well-known railroader with ties to the late \uc2 Hunter Harrison\uc2 , who is seen as the father of precision scheduled railroading. The unions didn't publicly defend \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 's then-CEO against \uc2 Soroban Capital Partners\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vena took over \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  in August.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw496\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7440\pichgoal7200
