\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Green Projects Surge, Jobs More Fleeting --- Employment drops after construction, and some ventures don't rely on U.S. aid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is pouring billions into regions at risk from the shift away from fossil fuels, hoping to create new, clean-energy jobs in places like Moapa, Nev.\par \par \uc2 Reid Gardner coal power plant that once employed as many as 300 people closed near this small town about an hour outside of Las Vegas in 2017. Nevada's public utility has since transformed the site into a home for batteries that store energy captured by nearby solar panels. The $257 million project received roughly $100 million in federal tax credits because of President Biden's Inflation Reduction Act, including a $25 million bonus for locating the project near a closed coal plant.\par \par \uc2 But the investment by \uc2 NV Energy\uc2 , a subsidiary of Warren Buffett's \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway Energy\uc2 , is set to fall short of the administration's vision for job creation and local economic development. Construction of the site and installation of the batteries required roughly 200 workers over a year. Maintaining and operating the batteries will require about five.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, those jobs likely would have been created even without the added tax break. \uc2 NV Energy\uc2  had planned to make the investment before the passage of the IRA, which gives companies an extra 10% of the cost of solar and wind projects located in "energy communities."\par \par \uc2 NV Energy\uc2  executives said the federal money will enable the utility to make new investments while keeping energy costs low for consumers across the state. "We can pass that benefit directly onto our customers in a time-efficient way," \uc2 NV Energy\uc2  Chief Executive Doug Cannon said.\par \par \uc2 The energy-community credit fuels a longstanding debate over the efficacy of "place-based policies," when governments use subsidies to try to create good-paying jobs in distressed areas so that residents don't have to move for better opportunities.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has embraced place-based economics, betting that clean-energy and advanced-technology incentives will narrow geographic inequality across the U.S. over time. Administration officials have been encouraged by a rush of investments into targeted energy communities, and they expect significant job creation in the long run.\par \par \uc2 "We're very focused on employment," National Economic Council Director \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2  said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Skeptics of place-based policies argue subsidies rarely accomplish their goals and primarily benefit wealthy investors and landowners. The energy-community credit's broad eligibility means many companies could receive extra tax breaks for investments they would have wanted to make without the support. And because the jobs created by solar and wind power are often concentrated in temporary construction, the benefits for targeted communities may be small or fleeting.\par \par \uc2 David Neumark\uc2 , an economist at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 , who studies location-based incentives, said a basic indicator of their efficacy is whether they create jobs "that would not have been created absent the credit. How do you avoid just paying firms money for what they would have done anyway?" he said.\par \par \uc2 When Kasey Davis, a longtime employee at Reid Gardner, first heard rumors about the closure of the coal plant in 2012, he was nervous. "It was like starting all over again," he said. Ultimately, disruption from the closure was minimal. He found a job at a nearby gas-fired power plant also run by \uc2 NV Energy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Davis's experience was typical. Jesse Newman, business manager of the local chapter of the International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers, said none of the workers at Reid Gardner lost their jobs when it closed; they either retired or transferred to one of \uc2 NV Energy\uc2 's other facilities. "It was actually a pretty good deal for the employees," he said.\par \par \uc2 Clean-energy investors and developers see the criteria for the energy-community bonus as relatively easy to meet. They include proximity to closed coal mines, coal power plants and areas where oil, natural gas or coal was an important industry, and the unemployment rate is at or above the national average.\par \par \uc2 That captures rust-belt areas in West Virginia and Pennsylvania, as well as regions that are natural magnets for solar or wind projects, including much of Texas and almost all of sun-soaked Nevada.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, companies announced $37.2 billion of investment across 204 projects that qualify for the bonus, according to Trevor Houser, a partner at the Rhodium Group. That is on top of 243 such projects, worth $21.2 billion, already under construction in energy communities last year.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  researchers estimated last year that clean-energy projects announced since the IRA would create 86,000 jobs, with 50,000 of those related to electric vehicles. But the administration's rhetoric about solar and wind jobs has at times frustrated some union officials, who see the work as less stable than traditional utility jobs. According to the Energy Department, 51% of solar jobs are in construction, while 3% involve continuing utility work afterward.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich532\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7980
