\par\par \b \uc2 IRS\uc2  Agents Shouldn't Work From Home\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Estes \par \uc2 569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, businesses relied on telework to continue operations. Now that the pandemic is over, businesses should be making data-driven decisions about remote work, and so should the federal government.\par \par \uc2 While some companies have flourished by allowing employees to work remotely, results are mixed. A 2023 Stanford Institute for Economic Policy Research paper cited several studies with wide-ranging findings on remote and hybrid work: productivity down 8% to 19% in one study, up 10% in another. With such inconsistent data, it's incumbent on individual businesses to determine how best to attract workers and meet customers' needs.\par \par \uc2 Enter the federal government, the largest employer in the U.S. with nearly three million employees. In a Feb. 15 House Ways and Means Committee hearing, I asked Daniel Werfel, commissioner of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , if all \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees had fully returned to the office after referencing a \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  report that found most federal agencies are using 25% or less of building capacity at their headquarters. "Everyone's working," he said. "It's 50% on-site versus 50% working in some remote location." He said this is in line with a governmentwide standard of 50% on-site work.\par \par \uc2 I asked because my office staff in Wichita, Kan., regularly assist constituents who are experiencing delayed responses and challenges when working with federal agencies. My office opened more than three times as many \uc2 IRS\uc2 -related cases in 2023 as in 2019, suggesting \uc2 IRS\uc2  staff were more effective at their jobs before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In the February hearing, I told Mr. Werfel about a car dealership in my district that had tried to file electronically its nonemployee compensation forms through the \uc2 IRS\uc2 's Information Returns Intake System. The portal repeatedly crashed while the dealership's staff tried to upload the forms, causing delays up to the Jan. 31 deadline. One woman tried calling the \uc2 IRIS\uc2  help desk several times but never got past the automated voice system.\par \par \uc2 It appears that our hearing struck a nerve with the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . News outlets including the Federal News Network reported that on the day of the hearing, Mr. Werfel emailed Washington-based \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees that they would need to return to the office for half of their workdays in any given month starting May 5: "Ultimately, to ensure ongoing alignment with the 50% on-site mandate for teleworkers, we need to take the necessary steps to increase our trend toward more on-site work."\par \par \uc2 Based on complaints my office has received, however, 50% may not be sufficient.\par \par \uc2 Taxpayers are stuck with delayed resolutions when the agency isn't as effective. The time a taxpayer spends on the phone, chasing paperwork or dealing with technical glitches takes away from productivity.\par \par \uc2 Some might argue that teleworking, with reduced overhead, offers a tax-saving benefit, but that's realized only if the agency is providing adequate customer service. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  isn't.\par \par \uc2 There's no alternative to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . Unlike a business with competition and the incentive to improve, it will likely continue to put higher priority on employees' comfort than on providing Americans with good, timely service. That means it is up to Congress to hold the agency accountable.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Estes, a Republican, represents Kansas' Fourth Congressional District.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Why I Want the \uc2 IRS\uc2  in Office" -- WSJ March 8, 2024)