\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Man Who Overheard Wife's Call Admits to Insider Trading --- Houston resident made $1.76 million using a nonpublic scoop of BP deal plan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gareth Vipers and Alyssa Lukpat \par \uc2 426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Interruptions from young children and the neighbor's noisy yard work are just some of the perils of remote work. For Tyler Loudon, it led to federal securities-fraud charges.\par \par \uc2 The Houston resident pleaded guilty this past week to insider trading after overhearing his wife, a former BP executive, discuss a planned acquisition while she was working from home.\par \par \uc2 Loudon made $1.76 million trading shares based on the details he heard, according to charges filed Thursday by the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After learning of BP's $1.3 billion deal to buy hundreds of U.S. convenience store and restaurant properties from \uc2 TravelCenters of America\uc2 , which had yet to be publicly announced, Loudon purchased more than 46,000 shares of TravelCenters stock without his wife's knowledge, the SEC alleges.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the roadside chain soared more than 70% after the agreement was announced.\par \par \uc2 "We allege that Mr. Loudon took advantage of his remote working conditions and his wife's trust to profit from information he knew was confidential," said Eric Werner, regional director of the SEC's Fort Worth regional office.\par \par \uc2 The episode is another instance of "pillow talk" securities fraud, in which insider traders glean confidential information from romantic partners. This kind of fraud is concerning for companies worried about their employees keeping information private when they work remotely.\par \par \uc2 Loudon also pleaded guilty to criminal charges from the U.S. attorney's office for the Southern District of Texas. Those charges carry a maximum sentence of up to five years in federal prison and fines up to $250,000. Sentencing is set for May 17.\par \par \uc2 Loudon confessed to his wife more than a month after he sold the stocks, according to a complaint the SEC filed in federal court. He said he wanted to make enough money so she didn't have to work long hours anymore. She moved out and later filed for divorce.\par \par \uc2 His wife was stunned by the revelation and reported it to her boss, according to the SEC. The company placed her on administrative leave and eventually fired her despite finding no evidence she knowingly leaked the acquisition to her husband.\par \par \uc2 BP declined to comment Friday. Loudon's lawyer didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Loudon didn't deny the allegations in the SEC complaint, but agreed to a partial judgment, subject to court approval.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240224ek2o0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
