\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- If These Resumes Could Talk: When Job Applicants Lie...and Get Busted --- Headhunters share their stories of workers who tried to fool their employers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [We ask recruiters and headhunters to share their wildest and most interesting stories.]\par \par \uc2 Q: What was an instance where candidates have resorted to deception or fraud?\par \par \uc2 'He's not who he says he is'\par \par \uc2 I placed an engineer with a large food-manufacturing company in North Carolina, and this client always did their own background check and vetting. We usually do that, but they liked to keep that separate.\par \par \uc2 Two years later, we got the call asking if we vetted this guy. We reminded them that they handled that process, and asked why.\par \par \uc2 Well, this guy's wife called the vice president of the company, and she said to the VP: "I just want you to know he's not who he says he is. He doesn't have an engineering degree. In fact, he never went to college at all."\par \par \uc2 He had been doing that job for two years! The company asked me to do some digging, and I come to find out he never went to that school, or any school. His references were all friends who'd made up fake names and titles.\par \par \uc2 What this guy was good at was reading things and BS-ing his way through it. But the way they caught him was they put him on this highly technical job and he just kept postponing the project. He was helping in the design of high-speed equipment -- and you can fake a lot of things in the business world but I'm not sure how you pull that off!\par \par \uc2 I don't know if the company ever would have caught him if his wife hadn't called -- they ended up getting divorced. God knows what else he lied about.\par \par \uc2 -- Al Polson\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Colonial Group\par \par \uc2 Losing two jobs at once\par \par \uc2 I placed a candidate as a vice president of sales at a company about 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 After he had been on the job for about six months, the company's executive vice president called me: "We had a real problem."\par \par \uc2 The VP of sales had a private office out of an executive suite here in Dallas, which provided an answering service. So he had a receptionist who would take his calls, and answer the phone: "John Doe, ABC Company."\par \par \uc2 One day, the executive vice president called the guy, and the receptionist answered with the right name but the wrong company: "John Doe, XYZ Company."\par \par \uc2 "Wait a minute, I'm looking for John Doe with ABC Company."\par \par \uc2 "Yes, that's right. I answer for that company, too."\par \par \uc2 "Wait, who do you answer for?"\par \par \uc2 "John Doe. He works for both ABC Company and XYZ Company."\par \par \uc2 "He does?"\par \par \uc2 "Yeah. I answer for both of them."\par \par \uc2 "What's the phone number for the second company he works for?"\par \par \uc2 The executive vice president called that second company, and once they figured out that this guy was working for both of them, the CEOs called him and fired him at the same time.\par \par \uc2 -- Tony Beshara\par \par \uc2 Babich & Associates\par \par \uc2 One person, four resumes\par \par \uc2 Over the years, I've had a candidate who has applied for numerous jobs at a hospitality company, using the same name and contact info, but four completely different resumes.\par \par \uc2 One resume might say they worked as a general manager at one hotel during a certain time, then another will say they worked for a different hotel during that time. At one point, I started looking up some of the company names that I didn't recognize to discover they didn't exist.\par \par \uc2 We actually talked to them about it -- we have point-blank told that individual, like, "Hey, I have multiple resumes on file for you, I'm not sure where you're working right now, and it's very difficult for me to trust your skills when I have this on file." But the applications from this person haven't stopped and neither have the changes to the resumes.\par \par \uc2 -- Sunny Larkin-Newman\par \par \uc2 Hospitality Ventures Management Group\par \par \uc2 Dead wrong\par \par \uc2 I had a candidate for a tech position who did really well in the interviews, was close to getting the job. When they did the background check and checked his Social Security number, it belonged to someone who had passed away about five years ago.\par \par \uc2 He was literally impersonating a dead person. We reached out to him about his Social Security number, and asked if there was some kind of mistake. He said he would call right back. We could never get in touch with him again -- and he disconnected his phone!\par \par \uc2 -- Anthony Fanzo\par \par \uc2 The Bachrach Group\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 These interviews have been edited and condensed for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240217ek2h0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
