\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Sweden Has a Caffeinated Secret to Happiness at Work --- Companies import the country's tradition of fika, a daily ritual of office-wide coffee breaks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Marie Chaker \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Would work be better if we all took a collective coffee break?\par \par \uc2 Workers in Sweden certainly think so. There, work life has long revolved around fika, a once- or twice-a-day ritual in which colleagues put away phones, laptops and any shoptalk to commune over coffee, pastries or other snacks. Swedish employees and their managers say the cultural tradition helps drive employee well-being, productivity and innovation by clearing the mind and fostering togetherness.\par \par \uc2 Now, as bosses and workers elsewhere try to reinvigorate office life and flagging job satisfaction, fika fascination is seeping into other workplaces.\par \par \uc2 The Grand, a New York-based career and leadership coaching platform, summons its all-remote staff of 10 every other Friday for coffee and conversation over Zoom. London-based Hubble, a website for finding flexible workspaces, took up the tradition after being introduced to it by a Swedish staff member.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone has an excuse to log off and let their hair down," said Tushar Agarwal, chief executive of Hubble, where staff gather the last Thursday of every month for baked goods, chitchat and, of course, coffee.\par \par \uc2 A recent product offering -- for part-time office space with new contract terms -- sprang from a discussion that took place during fika, says chief of staff Charlie Bastier. It's now one of the fastest-growing revenue streams, he says.\par \par \uc2 The pressure to make tweaks to the daily ritual is particularly acute in the U.S. Employees continue to report feeling less engaged in their jobs than in prepandemic times, Gallup data show.\par \par \uc2 In addition, bonding with colleagues has become harder and less of a priority for many people in the hybrid world of work. Some employers worry the lack of social cohesion is harming company culture and operations.\par \par \uc2 At The Grand's regular fika, staffers take turns hosting, leading with casual conversation or a board game such as Code Names or a drawing competition. The Grand's co-founder Rei Wang says that fika allows her to spend time with her staff, making her a better leader.\par \par \uc2 "Learning more about their passions and their geniuses helps me understand and collaborate with them," she says.\par \par \uc2 Pronounced "fee-kah," the Swedish culture of breaking for coffee involves much more than a schlep to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . It's meant to be a deliberate pause to provide space and time for people to connect. Many Swedish companies build a mandatory fika into the workday, while the Embassy of Sweden in Washington holds one for staff weekly. \uc2 IKEA\uc2 , promoting its Upphetta coffee maker on the corporate website, extols the virtues of fika: "When we disconnect for a short period, our productivity increases significantly."\par \par \uc2 "Fika is where we talk life, we talk everything but work itself," said Micael Dahlen, professor of well-being, welfare and happiness at the Stockholm School of Economics. The ritual helps drive trivsel, he says, a term that means a combination of workplace enjoyment and thriving. The concept is so fundamental to Swedish workplaces that many companies in Sweden have trivsel committees, he said.\par \par \uc2 Dahlen said he suspects a pandemic-era drop in office fikas contributed to a sharp decline in Swedes' happiness at work. Just over half of workers in Sweden reported a high level of job satisfaction in 2022, \uc2 according to Eurostat\uc2 , compared with 69.5% in 2017.\par \par \uc2 There's some evidence that communal coffee breaks help boost productivity. In a study of call-center workers at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , researchers at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2  found that teams that scheduled 15-minute breaks together were 18% more communicative with one another through the workday than groups with staggered breaks.\par \par \uc2 Annual turnover, likewise, was 12% among teams that held collective coffee breaks versus 40% among other workers. In all, the teamwork fostered via the breaks led to an estimated $15 million in increased annual productivity, says lead researcher Ben Waber, who has since founded a behavioral analytics company called Humanyze.\par \par \uc2 Hubble employees take turns baking and get a stipend of about $20 for supplies for the company's monthly fikas. Last week, 26 staff members gathered in a communal area away from desks and cubicles.\par \par \uc2 Kate Mehigan, an account manager, brought in homemade arancini balls and Eliot Dixon, an account team lead, laid out a Basque cheesecake from a recipe he'd found online. Some people played ping pong.\par \par \uc2 Fleur Sylvester, a Hubble account executive, used the time to quiz a colleague on training advice for running a half-marathon. Sylvester says when she joined the company over a year ago the gatherings were invaluable for helping put faces to names.\par \par \uc2 Peter Linder, head of thought leadership in North America for Swedish telecom giant \uc2 Ericsson\uc2 , recently introduced the fika concept to Jason Inskeep, senior director at management consulting company Slalom.\par \par \uc2 The two men had initially met on a joint panel discussion, and Linder wanted to congratulate Inskeep on his new job at Slalom. He sent Inskeep a Zoom invite for a 20-minute fika one-on-one.\par \par \uc2 "I didn't know what it was," Inskeep said.\par \par \uc2 The vibe of the midmorning conversation -- which meandered from the future of artificial intelligence to Inskeep's own feelings navigating a new company culture -- was different from the usual business tete-a-tete he said. Bouncing ideas back and forth in a relaxed way left him feeling energized the rest of the morning.\par \par \uc2 "It was a mix of coffee shop and barber shop," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240207ek2700007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
