\par\par \b \uc2 California Wage Law to Raise Fast-Food Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 California restaurants are some of the most expensive places to eat out in the country -- and they are about to get pricier.\par \par \uc2 Minimum wage for California fast-food workers is set to rise to $20 an hour in April, a 25% increase from the state's broader $16 minimum wage. Restaurants including \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, Chipotle, \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2  and others say they will raise menu prices in California in response, with some \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchisees estimating hundreds of thousands of dollars per restaurant in added labor costs.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone is going to have to pay more," said \uc2 Jack Hartung\uc2 , chief financial officer of California-based \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill\uc2 . Chipotle has raised its menu prices four times in the past two years and expects to increase them a further 5% to 9% in its California restaurants to cover the higher pay required for workers.\par \par \uc2 California for decades has been a fast-food stronghold, with more people working in restaurants and bars than in any other state, according to the Labor Department. It is the birthplace of \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , \uc2 In-N-Out Burger\uc2  and other chains, and fast-food and other takeaway restaurants employ about 761,900 people in the state, according to California's employment department.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, California passed a law instituting new oversight of the fast-food industry through state-appointed councils that could have raised sector pay to as much as $22 an hour. The restaurant industry dedicated tens of millions of dollars to invalidating the law before industry members, union representatives and the governor's office last year negotiated a compromise.\par \par \uc2 That deal set the new $20 minimum wage, and put a cap on annual increases beginning in 2025. California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , a Democrat, said the law would improve wages and working conditions for hundreds of thousands of fast-food employees in the state.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant owners since then have been calculating the cost. The National Owners Association, a group of \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchisees, estimated it will cost Golden Arches operators an additional $250,000 annually per restaurant, an amount that can't readily be absorbed, according to an email from the group last September.\par \par \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's Chief Executive \uc2 Chris Kempczinski\uc2  said in October that prices would go up at California restaurants as a result, but the company hadn't determined by how much. \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's said it is working with franchisees to help offset some of the added costs.\par \par \uc2 About 43% of \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2 's 2,200 restaurants are located in California, while nearly one-fifth of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's U.S. cafes are located in the state, according to industry research firm Technomic. Chipotle, \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2 , \uc2 KFC\uc2  and \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  also have at least 11% of their U.S. restaurants in California, Technomic said.\par \par \uc2 "There's certain areas in California which are going to have to go up a lot," \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  financial chief \uc2 Katherine Fogertey\uc2  said about menu prices in November.\par \par \uc2 For every dollar in hourly wage increases, a restaurant needs to increase prices by 2% to make up for the additional cost, industry consulting firm \uc2 Revenue Management Solutions\uc2  said. \uc2 Jack in the Box\uc2  told investors in November that its prices will rise 6% to 8% companywide this year, primarily to cover increased wages and other costs in California.\par \par \uc2 Some California consumers said they supported the idea of workers earning more but wished they didn't have to pay more to dine out. California restaurants already have some of the highest fast-food prices in the country, according to \uc2 Revenue Management Solutions\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "People are going to be able to eat out less and less. We are going to be eating at 99-cent stores," said Schirete Zick, a writer from Los Angeles who lives alone and prefers restaurants over filling her fridge with food that might spoil.\par \par \uc2 Economists have long debated whether minimum-wage increases help or hurt businesses, along with their impact on employment. A 2021 study by the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  found that raising the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour could lift hundreds of thousands of people out of poverty but could also cause prices to rise and cost 1.4 million Americans their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Few states or municipal areas have raised the minimum wage for workers to the degree that California is poised to do for fast-food employees. Localities typically increase minimum wages by 12% to 15% a year, according to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , which studies wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Fast-food prices already have been running high nationwide, and consumers are increasingly fatigued by the higher costs, leading to fewer restaurant visits or smaller orders, industry data shows.\par \par \uc2 "I am expecting to step on an escalator of ever-rising prices," said Andrew Strenk, a real-estate adviser from Tustin, Calif. He said he would likely eat out less often and hopes his health will improve as a result.\par \par \uc2 Besides raising prices, restaurants are striving to cut costs. Some chains, including \uc2 Burger King\uc2  and Denny's, said they hope that automating certain restaurant tasks can help their franchise owners avoid raising prices at California locations.\par \par \uc2 Burger King\uc2  aims to install more digital-ordering kiosks in its U.S. restaurants, with California now a focus, said \uc2 Josh Kobza\uc2 , chief executive of parent company \uc2 Restaurant Brands International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aaron Noveshen, founder of San Francisco-based chicken chain Starbird, said he wants to recruit franchisees to open new locations outside of California, given escalating wage, building and other costs.\par \par \uc2 "I do love it here, but there are a lot of forces that make it difficult to do business in California," Noveshen said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240205ek250001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
