\par\par \b \uc2 Jobs Growth Defies Expectations --- Employers added nearly twice as many positions as expected in January\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hiring is booming, defying expectations the economy would cool after going gangbusters last year.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 353,000 jobs last month, the Labor Department reported Friday. That was the strongest in a year and nearly double what economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal expected.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields surged after the report was released, a sign that investors believe the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might now take longer to cut interest rates. The S&P 500 climbed 1.1% to a record close, indicating that -- after months of falling inflation -- investors have become more confident that a strong labor market is sustainable rather than a problem that could cause prices to jump again.\par \par \uc2 December's payroll gains were also revised upward to 333,000 from 216,000, further undercutting the widely held view among economists and investors that it was becoming harder to find a job.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in January, forecast by economists to rise to 3.8%, instead held steady at 3.7%. Wages were up 4.5% from a year earlier, outpacing expectations, though the large increase may have reflected a big drop in hours worked -- a possible result of bad winter weather, some analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The report was pretty universally positive," said \uc2 Gennadiy Goldberg\uc2 , head of U.S. rates strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . "We've seen strong gains on headline payrolls, strong revisions and the unemployment rate staying unchanged. It is showing that the labor market is doing better than previously expected."\par \par \uc2 Friday's payroll report likely keeps the Fed on track to hold rates steady at its next meeting, March 19-20, as officials wait for more evidence that a recent trend of lower inflation continues.\par \par \uc2 The central bank left rates unchanged at their meeting on Wednesday, and Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said most officials expect to cut rates this year if lower inflation readings continues. But he suggested they weren't likely to be confident enough about the sustainability of lower inflation by the time of their next meeting.\par \par \uc2 "This is a good situation. Let's be honest. This is a good economy," Powell said on Wednesday at a news conference. "We do expect growth to moderate. Of course, we have expected it for some time, and it hasn't happened."\par \par \uc2 Before Friday, recent data had shown the labor market gradually cooling, with wage growth easing and the pace of hiring moderating. Economists surveyed by the Journal last month expected payrolls to grow a paltry 64,000 a month this year, after averaging 255,000 a month last year.\par \par \uc2 The bulk of hiring last year came from just three sectors: government, healthcare and restaurants and hotels. In January, however, job gains broadened, with nearly two-thirds of private-sector industries adding to their payroll or keeping them steady.\par \par \uc2 The labor force grew 124,000 after accounting for the annual change to the Labor Department's estimate of the underlying population, the Labor Department said. Without that adjustment, the labor force showed a decline. The participation rate in January was unchanged at 62.5%, with the population revisions having no effect.\par \par \uc2 The Fed last year raised rates to the highest level in more than 20 years, but the economy, far from falling into recession, has grown at a brisk pace. The Fed's preferred inflation gauge was 2.6% in December, not far from the central bank's 2% goal.\par \par \uc2 Jobs reports for January have been somewhat hard to read since the onset of the pandemic. In the past two years, gains were well above what economists had expected, but there was speculation that the numbers might have been exaggerated by shifts in seasonal hiring patterns.\par \par \uc2 Few analysts, though, were quibbling with the basic message of Friday's data.\par \par \uc2 When the Fed started raising rates in early 2022, many investors and economists thought that a surge in unemployment was nearly inevitable. Fed officials themselves said that slower growth and a weaker jobs market were likely needed to curb inflation and that an economic slowdown was a serious possibility.\par \par \uc2 Unexpected as Friday's report was, investors have almost become used to such surprises after months of data showing inflation falling rapidly and economic growth stable or accelerating.\par \par \uc2 Economists have no one explanation, but certain trends have gone as the Fed hoped. In particular, higher rates seemed to have cooled demand for workers without a big jump in layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Job openings have declined and more workers are staying in their current jobs instead of quitting in hopes of better pay.\par \par \uc2 That has helped bring down wage growth, which Fed officials see as critical to tempering overall inflation. In addition, businesses have been able to increase the supply of goods to meet still-high consumer demand, aided by an influx of workers returning to their labor force.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich379\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5685
