\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Heard on the Street: Where Are All the New Workers Coming From? --- Discrepancies in the data mean the U.S. might have more room to add jobs than previously thought\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 January's surprisingly strong jobs numbers pose a puzzle: If the economy is generating jobs at such a rapid pace, where are employers finding the people to fill them?\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Friday reported that the U.S. gained a seasonally adjusted 353,000 jobs in January -- nearly double what economists expected -- and additionally boosted the previous two month's job gains by a total of 126,000. With that, the economy added an average of 289,000 jobs over the past three months -- the swiftest pace of job creation since last year's first quarter. The unemployment rate remained at a low 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 It is striking that after tacking on 2.9 million jobs over the past 12 months, the job market isn't even tighter. Indeed, at 3.7%, the unemployment rate in January was higher than the multidecade low of 3.4% it logged in January of last year. Nor does this appear to be a case of more people coming off the sidelines to enter the job market: The labor-force participation rate -- the share of the working-age population employed or seeking employment -- was 62.5% last month versus 62.4% a year earlier. So where is the extra slack coming from?\par \par \uc2 The disconnect appears to be a tale of two surveys. The jobs figures come from a survey of nonfarm employers, while the unemployment and labor-force participation rate comes from a survey of households. The household survey has been showing much slower employment gains than the employer survey. Indeed, in annual revisions included in Friday's report, the was a slight reduction in the working-age population. That led to a decrease in the household survey's employment level.\par \par \uc2 "Household employment appears to have grown at less than one-third the pace of payroll gains, which makes no sense whatsoever," said Robert Barbera, director for the Center for Financial Economics at \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is possible that the household survey is giving the more accurate read on employment. But considering the strong job-market signals coming from other sources, such as the weekly initial-jobless-claims figures, that seems unlikely.\par \par \uc2 What might be happening instead is that the Census Bureau data that the Labor Department relies on underestimated how quickly the population is growing, and that has led it to undercount employment in the household survey. In an update last month, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimated that the U.S. population rose by 0.9% in 2023 from a year earlier, versus a Census estimate of 0.5% -- a difference driven by the CBO's much larger estimate of net immigration to the U.S. last year. If that is the case, expansion of the labor supply through immigration should make it possible to grow the economy faster without triggering inflation.\par \par \uc2 Yet some uncertainty remains. While it is possible to come up with explanations for the discrepancy, the complication for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , and investors, is that the crossed signals make it hard to tell not just how quickly the job market is growing, but how quickly it can grow without overheating.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240203ek230001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
