\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Remote Workers Feel Brunt of Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 861 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Laying off employees is complicated. New data suggests that managers tend to have an easier time showing remote workers the door.\par \par \uc2 Workers logging on from home five days a week were 35% more likely to be laid off in 2023 than their peers who put in office time, according to an analysis of two million white-collar workers conducted by employment data provider Live Data Technologies. The analysis showed 10% of fully remote workers were laid off last year, compared with 7% of those working in an office full time or on a hybrid basis.\par \par \uc2 "When a hiring manager gets news they have to cut 10% of the staff, it's easier to put someone on the list you don't have a close personal relationship with," says Andy Challenger, senior vice president at \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , an outplacement firm. Much of the disparity, he says, is that it's simply harder to build attachments to people you don't see face to face.\par \par \uc2 Some companies have been more intentional about putting remote workers on the chopping block. \uc2 Wayfair\uc2 , the online home-goods retailer, recently told employees that remote workers would be more likely to be affected in company layoffs. Executives also told staff they believe most workers should be in an office most days.\par \par \uc2 "We went overboard in hiring during a strong economic period and veered away from our core principles," Chief Executive \uc2 Niraj Shah\uc2  wrote in an earlier staff memo. \uc2 Wayfair\uc2  said while the company believes its best work is done in person, it will continue to have remote roles when warranted.\par \par \uc2 Given that many layoffs during the past year have been concentrated in tech -- a remote-friendly industry that overhired during the pandemic -- it's not surprising that people working exclusively from home have been harder hit, says George Penn, a managing vice president at \uc2 Gartner\uc2 , an advisory firm, who consults with companies on workforce restructuring.\par \par \uc2 Other managerial perceptions could be at play, too, he adds. A \uc2 Gartner\uc2  survey in 2021 found that 68% of executives and managers believed in-office workers were higher performers than remote employees.\par \par \uc2 In Albuquerque, N.M., Liana Robertson, 56 years old, who was laid off this month from her client support job, says she struggled in her fully remote role to get on the same page as her manager.\par \par \uc2 "Bosses don't think you're actually doing work, because they can't see what you're doing," she says. "They assume everything takes 10 minutes, and why haven't you finished it?"\par \par \uc2 Ron Porter, a \uc2 Korn Ferry\uc2  consultant on workforce issues, says he is hearing from clients that fully remote workers are getting cut more frequently than their in-office and hybrid colleagues. Part of it, he says, is natural human reluctance managers feel to cut those they have to face in person. It's also possible that workers who are remote 100% of the time may be more marginal to start with, he adds, with managers preferring to keep their strongest employees doing the most important tasks close to them at the office when possible.\par \par \uc2 Alyssa Ciesky, 40, a talent acquisition specialist based in San Antonio, says that as a fully remote worker, she's aware of the risks. "They're easy to get rid of, you can't put a face to the name, there's no personal connection." she says of workers like herself. When Ciesky was laid off at the end of 2022 from her fully remote role at a semiconductor company, she says she wasn't surprised.\par \par \uc2 Still, she says that as a mother of two young children, she's willing to take on the prospect of instability for the flexibility of remote work.\par \par \uc2 "It's worth it," she says, adding that she also likes the feeling of autonomy and the ability to focus that working from home offers.\par \par \uc2 David Risch, a recruiter in the supply-chain industry, says in today's weaker job market, he cautions candidates to keep their eyes open with all-remote roles.\par \par \uc2 "We warn them, easy come, easy go," he says. "A company's going to invest in someone who's investing in them."\par \par \uc2 Remote workers are also more likely to quit. According to Live Data Technologies, 12% of fully remote workers left and began a new job within two months in 2023, compared with 9% of those who worked on a hybrid or in-person basis.\par \par \uc2 Some CEOs say that while they personally don't look at an employee's remote-work status when determining job cuts, they understand why work-from-home status could put them at higher risk. Other data and studies indicate that executives are rewarding in-office employees with promotions and favorable assignments.\par \par \uc2 "If you have a person working in finance who's not coming to the office, why wouldn't you hire that same person in India or in the Philippines?" said \uc2 Christian Ulbrich\uc2 , chief executive of real-estate company \uc2 Jones Lang LaSalle\uc2 , noting that in many cases overseas workers can do the same job for less money.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Chip Cutter contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240130ek1u0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
