\par\par \b \uc2 Life Science: Death, Taxes and Bailouts in \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's California\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allysia Finley \par \uc2 858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  spent last week traipsing about South Carolina, ostensibly stumping for Joe Biden. While it may seem he's running a shadow campaign because he's overshadowing the 81-year-old president, he may also simply be trying to get out of Dodge.\par \par \uc2 It isn't hard to understand why he would be looking for a parachute as his home state careens toward a hard landing. Unemployment is climbing as hordes of workers and businesses leave. The state faces an estimated $68 billion budget deficit this coming year. Cities are bleeding red ink as their labor costs swell and tax revenue shrinks.\par \par \uc2 Politicians in California like to boast that the state is the world's fifth-largest economy and a national bellwether. Mr. Biden can only hope that as California goes, the rest of the country doesn't follow.\par \par \uc2 Employment in the Golden State has fallen by 180,520 since June while its jobless rate has climbed to 5.1% from 4.1% a year ago. The national unemployment rate is 3.7% and even lower in Florida (3%), where employment has grown 111,433 over the last six months.\par \par \uc2 After padding their workforces during the pandemic, tech and finance companies are laying off workers or shifting them to other states. So are manufacturers laboring under the state's climate regulations. Speculative startups are struggling to raise private capital amid higher interest rates, which have also chilled the market for initial public offerings.\par \par \uc2 Venture investors are having to write down their shares, though the Biden administration's guarantee of uninsured deposits at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  last spring spared them from bigger losses. While stock prices surged last autumn on hopes that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would begin cutting interest rates, California's tax collections have gone sideways.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 index closed last year 24% higher than the year before. Yet California's personal income and corporate tax revenues in December were 3.3% and 21% lower, respectively. Stock prices have been this stretched only during the dot-com bubble, 2001 recession, financial crisis and early in the pandemic, so a market correction may be inexorable. Look out below.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the state has blown through tens of billions of dollars in federal pandemic largess, expanded Medicaid to undocumented immigrants, and approved new labor agreements with generous pay increases for government workers including prison guards. California spends $132,860 annually per prisoner, up 90% from a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Because government employee pensions are based on compensation at the end of their careers, pay increases inflate the state's pension costs. Sacramento's required pension contribution for a highway-patrol officer now equals nearly 68% of compensation, meaning that this year the state must pay about $68,000 into the pension fund for an officer earning $100,000.\par \par \uc2 As awful as the budget outlook is in Sacramento, it may be worse for cities across the state, which have few options other than to cut essential services or raise taxes. The city manager in Sacramento recently suggested patching a $50 million budget gap by taking longer to fix potholes and broken street lights.\par \par \uc2 San Diego\uc2  projects a $172 million budget shortfall. "Without a new major ongoing revenue source, significant budget cuts to programs and services will be needed to address the city's structural budget deficit," a Dec. 5 budget report stated.\par \par \uc2 Sales tax revenue is declining while federal pandemic aid has dried up. Although retail spending nationwide continues to grow faster than pre-pandemic levels, Californians have less money to spend at stores and restaurants because they are having to shell out more on gasoline, electricity and home and auto insurance. Blame the state's progressive policies.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles City Administrator Matthew W. Szabo recently warned of a $400 million deficit owing to rising labor costs and lower-than-expected tax revenue. "The identification of new revenue sources is one strategy that the City must embrace in order to become more resilient," Mr. Szabo wrote in a Jan. 19 report to the City Council.\par \par \uc2 The trouble is, taxpayers in La La Land are as maxed out as those in the rest of California. San Francisco faces a nearly $800 million deficit despite having imposed several new taxes in recent years, including one on the gross receipts of businesses whose highest-paid manager earns more than 100 times the median compensation of its San Francisco-based employees.\par \par \uc2 This tax is layered on another new gross receipts tax on business revenue in San Francisco above $50 million and a tax on commercial rents. All these taxes punish businesses with offices and workers in the city, which is why so many, such as \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2 , have said sayonara. It's the same story up and down California.\par \par \uc2 There's much ruin in a state, to adapt Adam Smith, and California seems intent on proving it. Regardless of whether Mr. Newsom fails up to the Oval Office, you can bet that California politicians will soon be importuning for another Washington rescue. The only certainties in California are death, taxes and bailouts.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240129ek1t0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
