\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: State Labor Markets Offer Mixed Picture\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gwynn Guilford \par \uc2 443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. labor market closed out 2023 looking strong. But the picture is more mixed at the state level.\par \par \uc2 Some 30 states added jobs last year, while employment stayed flat in the remaining states and the District of Columbia, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Some Mountain states had a particularly strong showing: Nevada payrolls jumped 3.8% in December from a year earlier, while employment in Idaho and Wyoming rose by around 3%.\par \par \uc2 Texas saw the biggest gains in absolute terms, adding nearly 370,000 to payrolls in 2023, an increase of 2.7%. The state has added jobs in 44 of the past 45 months.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rates rose in December compared with a year earlier in 18 states and the District of Columbia, while 15 states saw declines. The rest were unchanged. The share of states whose unemployment rates increased in the final three months of 2023 declined, according to \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  economists.\par \par \uc2 With fewer than 8,000 North Dakotans looking for work in December, the state ended 2023 with its lowest unemployment rate on record, at 1.9%. Unemployment rates in Pennsylvania and South Dakota finished the year near record lows, too.\par \par \uc2 Maryland also saw just 1.9% of its labor force unemployed last month, though that was up slightly from the 1.6% all-time nadir reached in September of last year -- the lowest monthly jobless rate of any state since the series began in 1976.\par \par \uc2 Things were grimmer in New Jersey, whose jobless rate jumped to 4.8% in December, from 3.3% a year earlier, as employers in professional and business services and trade, transportation and utilities shed jobs.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rates in California leapt to 5.1%, up from the low-4% range in December 2022, even as employers there added more than 310,000 jobs last year. Employment in the Golden State took a hit from job losses in financial activities and professional and business services, continuing fallout from overzealous pandemic hiring in the tech industry and banking turmoil early last year.\par \par \uc2 Labor-force growth also diverged. Workers flooded into local labor markets throughout the South in 2023, with the labor forces in Oklahoma, South Carolina, Texas and Florida all expanding at a higher rate than the 1.5% increase nationwide. In the Midwest, the share of Michiganders working or looking for work also surpassed the national labor-force rate of increase.\par \par \uc2 However, the labor forces of New Hampshire and Oregon, at best, grew more slowly than the national pace, and -- due to a fairly wide margin of error -- may have outright shrunk.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich342\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal5130
