\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Educated Workforce Boosts New Hampshire --- Growth is slower than U.S. as a whole, but joblessness was 2.3% in November\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 419 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New Hampshire, the site of Tuesday's Republican primary, is growing more slowly than the U.S. as a whole but benefits from an ultralow unemployment rate and an educated population.\par \par \uc2 The Granite State, home to 1.4 million people, grew at a 4.5% annual rate in the third quarter, ranking it 31st out of 50 states in gross domestic product. The U.S. economy grew at a 4.9% rate in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 Important industries in New Hampshire include advanced manufacturing, healthcare, tech and tourism, and its low tax rates attract foreign direct investment, economists say.\par \par \uc2 The state's unemployment rate is significantly lower than the national average, and it stood at 2.3% in November compared with 3.7% nationwide in December. It tied with Nebraska for the fifth-lowest jobless rate in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire's low unemployment is partly a function of its highly educated workforce -- 41.3% of people have at least a bachelor's degree in New Hampshire, compared with 35.7% nationwide -- and a plentiful supply of jobs in education and health services, trade, manufacturing and professional and business services.\par \par \uc2 Workers in New Hampshire also earn more than the national average because of a scarcity of skilled workers and the high cost of living in the state.\par \par \uc2 Workforce constraints are a major issue for New Hampshire's future ability to grow, according to Jennifer Kelber, associate professor of economics and business at Saint Anselm College in Manchester.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire's population skews older than the nation's, and the state has the sixth-lowest fertility rate, according to the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. has seen its labor-force participation rate -- the share of adults working or looking for a job -- recover in recent years after a large drop during the Covid-19 pandemic, New Hampshire's plunged during the pandemic and has been on a downward trend ever since.\par \par \uc2 Affordable child care is in short supply, another crimp on the labor force, and housing costs are above the national average. Factors such as supply constraints and homeowners' unwillingness to sell their properties and give up low interest rates are behind the lack of affordable housing, said Kelber, as well as strong demand for second homes in lake and mountain areas popular for tourism.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6645
