\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News --- THE OUTLOOK: Inflation Battle Stands to Lose Key Lever\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe \par \uc2 871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many workers came off the sidelines to join the labor force in 2022 and 2023, aiding the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's fight to tame inflation by easing labor shortages and putting downward pressure on wage growth.\par \par \uc2 Big additional gains may be hard to achieve.\par \par \uc2 That matters because inflation remains above the Fed's 2% target despite a major retreat last year, and policymakers would like to see continued progress before relenting on their restrictive policy stance.\par \par \uc2 Inflation cooled in part because of improvements on the supply side of the economy. Supply chains continued to heal after Covid-19 pandemic disruptions and employers found more workers available for hire. The result: The economy defied expectations that a pullback in inflation would come at a great economic cost. Instead, growth surged as consumer demand endured and employers added jobs.\par \par \uc2 That win-win scenario could become harder to sustain without additional supply gains, because a further cooling in inflation might require an easing in demand, which could hold down growth.\par \par \uc2 Fed policymakers are considering the question of how much more supply the economy has to offer. Several "assessed that healing in supply chains and labor supply was largely complete, and therefore that continued progress in reducing inflation may need to come mainly from further softening in product and labor demand," according to minutes of their December policy meeting. (A few officials assessed there is still room for additional supply gains.)\par \par \uc2 The labor force, those who have or are looking for work, plunged by more than eight million people at the start of the pandemic, according to the Labor Department, as the economy fell into a short but deep recession. The participation rate, or the share of Americans who are in the labor force, fell more than 3 percentage points from February to April 2020.\par \par \uc2 The labor force began to rebound that spring, with great strides during 2022 and 2023 as the pandemic receded. It grew by roughly 2.4 million people last year, after adding 2.6 million in 2022. Those were larger gains than in 2019, just before the pandemic. The increases last year were concentrated in the first eight months, boosted by an influx of prime-age workers -- those age 25 to 54 -- immigrants and others who had been sidelined for reasons related to the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 While the participation rate is still below prepandemic levels, it was 62.5% in December compared with 60.1% in April 2020.\par \par \uc2 But the labor force contracted in the last four months of 2023, while employers continued to add jobs. The participation rate has essentially held steady since August. The rate for prime-age workers, while remaining near the highest levels in roughly two decades, has fallen since September. Many older workers fled the labor force at the onset of the pandemic and haven't returned. The participation rate for those 55 and older remains nearly 2 percentage points below its February 2020 level.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has been closely watching wage growth as one gauge of price pressures in the economy. While strong pay raises are a boon to workers, they can feed additional inflation if employers pass on their costs to customers.\par \par \uc2 Employers in 2022 and 2023 offered robust pay raises to lure a limited pool of workers for a high number of openings. Women and Americans who may face barriers to employment were among the groups that responded, joining the labor force in greater numbers. Researchers at the \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  found the strong labor market during the pandemic recovery led to especially large gains in labor-force participation and employment rates for prime-age Black people.\par \par \uc2 The foreign-born share of workers in the labor force climbed to historic highs, as immigration backlogs that developed during the Trump administration and early in the pandemic eased.\par \par \uc2 As labor shortages decreased, wage growth and demand for workers softened, helping wring out some of the inflationary pressures. Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  has pointed to improving labor supply as one reason the economy performed well despite interest-rate increases aimed to slow it.\par \par \uc2 Some of the cyclical factors that drew people in last year are fading as pay raises and job growth slow, said \uc2 Gus Faucher\uc2 , chief economist at PNC.\par \par \uc2 "Most of the people who were drawn into that strong labor market have already come into the labor force," Faucher said. That, combined with baby boomer retirements, could mean a much slower pace of labor force growth this year.\par \par \uc2 He estimates the labor force will grow by 850,000 during 2024 and the participation rate will be 62.7% at the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 If a pullback in demand for workers, rather than a boost in supply, helps push inflation even lower, there could be a greater impact on growth than has been the case so far, said \uc2 Sarah House\uc2 , a senior economist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That means there's less need to bring on additional workers, fewer people getting a paycheck, and fewer people have money to go out and put back into the economy," she said. "Supply is the benign way out of inflationary pressures coming from the labor market."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich297\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4455
