\par\par \b \uc2 Job Gains Endure In Cooling Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,001 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers hired at a solid pace in December, capping a year of steady gains for a job market that continues to defy expectations and remains a bright spot in a gradually cooling economy.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy added 216,000 jobs last month with most industries increasing employment, the Labor Department reported Friday. That was larger than November's gain of 173,000, and better than forecasters were expecting. For all of 2023, employers added 2.7 million jobs, a slowdown from 4.8 million in 2022 but a better gain than in the several years preceding the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Wages rose a healthy 4.1% last month from a year earlier, and the unemployment rate in December held at 3.7%. The jobless rate began 2023 at 3.4%, matching lows not seen since the late 1960s.\par \par \uc2 The labor market's slowing but steady pace in 2023, coupled with a sharp slowdown in inflation, has fueled optimism that the economy can achieve a so-called soft landing. That would mean inflation eases without a recession.\par \par \uc2 Major stock indexes rose slightly Friday following the jobs report, though they were down for the first week of 2024. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 26 points on Friday and ended the week down 223 points, or 0.59%. The S&P 500 Index fell 73 points, or 1.52%, this past week. The yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note rose on Friday and was up 0.18 percentage point this past week to 4.04%.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have signaled they are comfortable for now maintaining their benchmark federal-funds rate at a 23-year high as they await more evidence that inflation is falling back to their 2% target. At their meeting last month, most officials penciled in at least three rate cuts this year, a sign the central bank is done lifting rates.\par \par \uc2 Friday's report will do little to alter that calculus and keeps the central bank on track to hold rates steady at its next policy meeting, Jan. 30-31. It suggests the labor market is no longer at risk of overheating, particularly as private-sector hiring has eased over the past few months. It also doesn't point to material weakness that might move up the Fed's timetable for cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 "It's a labor market that showed substantial resilience while cooling to levels that were much more acceptable from the Fed's perspective," said Matthew Luzzetti, chief U.S. economist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "It was about as good of an outcome for the labor market as you could have hoped for in 2023."\par \par \uc2 As many companies pull back on hiring, others see a chance to snap up workers who might have once been tougher to recruit.\par \par \uc2 Industrial real-estate giant \uc2 Prologis\uc2  is looking to hire engineering managers and salespeople to help as the company adds electric vehicle charging stations and solar power to more of its sites.\par \par \uc2 The company is finding that applications for open positions are up and that candidates are looking for an employer that can offer stability, said Nathaalie Carey, a senior vice president of human resources at \uc2 Prologis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are pretty bullish," she said. "There have been these opportunities to find talent, maybe in places we haven't necessarily appealed to in the past, and now we're able to really go after that talent."\par \par \uc2 At the start of 2023, the job market and broader economy were widely expected to slow substantially, the anticipated result of the Fed's aggressive rate-hiking campaign. Instead, strong consumer spending and an increase in available workers helped underpin the economy this past year, allowing hiring to chug along as labor shortages receded.\par \par \uc2 There are signs of cooling.\par \par \uc2 Employers added a combined 71,000 fewer jobs in October and November than previously estimated. Other data showed open positions fell at the end of 2023 compared with the start, and workers are quitting their jobs at a lower rate than before the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Economic activity at service-industry businesses decelerated in December, as employment contracted sharply, the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Yet, unemployment and layoffs remain low and vacancies are higher than 2019 levels, suggesting demand for workers is still solid.\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers have long since replaced all the jobs lost early in the pandemic. Still, the employment level late in 2023 was below the trend if hiring had continued at the same pace as the decade before the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Hiring in 2023 was concentrated in a handful of industries: Gains in the leisure and hospitality, government and healthcare sectors together accounted for the bulk of job creation in 2023, according to Labor Department data.\par \par \uc2 That is another signal the labor market is losing some steam, said \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , U.S. lead economist at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare hiring continues to be boosted in part by structural forces, such as the aging of the U.S. population. Employers in the public sector and in leisure and hospitality continue to play catch-up from disruptions during the pandemic. Employment in leisure and hospitality rose in December and is close to its level in early 2020.\par \par \uc2 Forecasters expect a continued cooling in wage growth and a drawdown of pandemic-era savings to cause consumers to pull back on spending in 2024. That, in turn, could slow the labor market and economic growth overall. Fed policymakers, for example, expect the jobless rate to rise to 4.1% by the fourth quarter and the economy to grow at a slower rate this year compared with the year before.\par \par \uc2 Many economists have lowered their estimates of the probability the economy will see a recession, however.\par \par \uc2 "The way it's played out, we've kind of had an economy where we've had our cake and been able to eat it too," said \uc2 Jonathan Millar\uc2 , senior economist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Nick Timiraos contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw251\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3765\pichgoal9600
