\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Companies Remain Reluctant to Lay Off Employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 A lot of people spent much of last year convinced that the U.S. was on the cusp of a recession -- including those in charge of hiring and firing workers.\par \par \uc2 Lately, though, with the economy continuing to grow even as inflation has been coming down, those recession worries have moderated. This might not lead to a reacceleration of job growth, but it could set the conditions for the labor market to not cool as much over the next year as many economists and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policymakers expect.\par \par \uc2 Economists have been lowering their estimates of the chances of a recession, and so are American companies. In the fourth quarter, 239 transcripts of earnings calls included the word "recession" at least once, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . This was the fewest since the first quarter of 2022, when the Fed had only recently begun its series of rate increases, and well below 790 logged in 2022's fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 What is curious is that even as companies complained about a coming recession, the economy kept adding jobs. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expect Friday's employment report to show a gain of 170,000 jobs in December from November. That would equate to a gain of 2.7 million in 2023 -- fewer than 2022's 4.8 million but, outside of the pandemic period, the strongest year for job growth since 2014.\par \par \uc2 One reason for this might be that even though companies thought the economy's prospects were bad, they didn't judge their own prospects so poorly. A survey-based index compiled by \uc2 Duke University\uc2  and the Federal Reserve Banks of Atlanta and Richmond shows that CFOs' optimism about the U.S. economy fell sharply in 2022 and only recently started showing signs of recovery. But their optimism about their own companies didn't take much of a hit.\par \par \uc2 For employers, shedding workers when the business is doing well, even if they think a recession is coming, usually isn't wise. If the economy defies expectations, refilling jobs can be challenging. This could be part of what is behind the "labor hoarding" dynamic that has been in place in which employers have been reluctant to let workers go. The Labor Department on Wednesday reported that a seasonally adjusted 1.5 million people were laid off in November. In 2019 -- a good year for the job market -- an average of 1.8 million people were laid off each month.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Wednesday's report also showed that the fewest people were hired in November, outside of the job collapse early in the pandemic, since December 2017. Hiring still eclipsed combined layoffs, quits, retirements and other job separations, so employment expanded. But it doesn't look as if companies are racing to increase their workforces.\par \par \uc2 The job market will face challenges this year beyond economic perceptions. All those Fed rate hikes are weighing on at least some businesses, for example, while the hiring needs in sectors that lost a lot of workers during the pandemic, such as healthcare, have probably become a bit less severe.\par \par \uc2 But if recession worries continue to fade, more employers might move beyond trying to hang on to the workers they already have to trying to bring new ones into the fold. This could help the job market keep its footing. It also could stymie investors' expectations that the Fed will start cutting rates as soon as March.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich258\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal3870
