\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Sales Of Autos Bounced Back In 2023 --- Carmakers are expected to see demand level off this year after rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 1,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. auto industry rebounded in 2023 with many car companies reporting double-digit sales gains, marking a return to normalcy for a sector that has been on a roller coaster since the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Automakers' results were boosted by pent-up demand and better availability on dealership lots. A six-week \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike last fall did little to damp the industry's momentum, and electric-vehicle sales continued to rise, albeit at a slower rate than in the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Industrywide sales of new cars in the U.S. reached 15.5 million vehicles in 2023, a 12.4% increase from the prior year, according to an estimate from research firm Wards Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Of those automakers that reported sales figures Wednesday, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  said it sold 2.6 million vehicles in the U.S. in 2023, retaining its crown as the nation's largest car seller. Those sales were a 14% increase over the prior year.\par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 's U.S. sales increased nearly 7% in 2023 to about 2.3 million, lifted by sales of plug-in models, while American Honda Motor recorded a 33% increase for the period. \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  plans to report its full-year sales results on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company of Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram, was one outlier, reporting a 1% decline in U.S. sales last year.\par \par \uc2 Supply-chain snags, manufacturing disruptions and poorly stocked dealerships have weighed on new-vehicle sales since the beginning of the Covid-19 health crisis, leading to the industry's worst sales year in more than a decade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Now, consumers are finding more options at dealerships as some of the snarls that impeded factory production in recent years have eased. Increasing inventory levels have led to moderating new-vehicle prices and more promotional deals, providing a welcome relief for car shoppers who have dealt with sharply increasing price tags.\par \par \uc2 "We're finally getting back to the traditional buyer's market," said Cole Potamkin, chief operating officer at Potamkin Cos., which runs a chain of dealerships across the country.\par \par \uc2 But this year could prove more challenging for automakers as stock levels continue to rise and higher interest rates stretch more U.S. car buyers financially.\par \par \uc2 Carmakers, too, have enjoyed several years of robust profits, partly because of consumers willing to pay more for vehicles that were in short supply.\par \par \uc2 Now they are confronting a more hesitant group with some auto executives saying new-car prices will drop further this year.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be monthly hand-to-hand combat versus our key competitors," said Randy Parker, chief executive at \uc2 Hyundai Motor America\uc2 . "2024 no doubt is going to be a very challenging year."\par \par \uc2 The months ahead could also test the industry's resolve to stock dealerships with fewer vehicles on a more permanent basis.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the pandemic, auto executives had pledged to keep inventories tight as a way to maximize profits, a sign that industrywide sales aren't likely to return to prepandemic levels where results for several years topped 17 million vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The growth rate is expected to level out this year, with \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2  projecting about 15.6 million in annual sales, a minimal increase from last year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, automakers are rushing to build new U.S. factories and expand manufacturing output, in part to support their longer-term transition to EVs.\par \par \uc2 While car prices remain far above what they were in 2019, the average price paid for a new vehicle peaked in December 2022 and fell further last year to about $46,055 last month, according to research firm \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike dented production at the Detroit Three last fall, but because they had built up supplies heading into talks, it had limited impact on sales.\par \par \uc2 Still, labor rates are going up for many automakers. The new \uc2 UAW\uc2  contracts in Detroit secured double-digit pay increases and other benefits for auto workers that are poised to add billions of dollars in new costs for \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2  and several other nonunionized rivals have also boosted pay for their workers, in the wake of the union deals.\par \par \uc2 Electric-vehicle sales in the U.S. continued to rise in 2023, up 48% for the first 11 months of the year, according to Motor Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 But the pace of growth also slowed from the prior year, resulting in several car companies walking back or delaying some of their planned investment in EVs last year.\par \par \uc2 High sticker prices on electric vehicles, along with a lack of public chargers and fears of getting stranded, have made buyers hesitant to make the switch from a gasoline-engine vehicle, dealers and analysts have said.\par \par \uc2 Electric-car pioneer \uc2 Tesla\uc2  reported on Tuesday global vehicle deliveries of about 1.8 million EVs worldwide in 2023, slightly ahead of analysts' expectations.\par \par \uc2 However, despite steep price cuts, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's growth rate slowed last year and Chinese automaker BYD surpassed it in the fourth quarter as the world's largest EV seller.\par \par \uc2 Rival EV maker \uc2 Rivian Automotive\uc2  said on Tuesday it delivered 50,122 EVs last year, missing Wall Street's expectations. The company's vehicle sales for the fourth quarter fell 10% from the third quarter, despite an increase in factory output.\par \par \uc2 Another obstacle for carmakers and potential buyers is the Biden administration's recently defined rules on which electric models qualify for a $7,500 tax credit.\par \par \uc2 Vehicles that are assembled using battery parts from "a foreign entity of concern," including China, aren't eligible for the credit starting this year.\par \par \uc2 A number of popular electric vehicles, including Ford's Mustang Mach-E and some \uc2 Tesla\uc2  models, lost their eligibility for the tax credit Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 For customers reluctant to go fully electric, hybrids have become a more attractive option, and 2024 is expected to be another strong year for sales of these electrified models, auto executives said.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is aware that the hybrid is sort of the darling of the industry right now," said Mamadou Diallo, head of auto sales at American Honda Motor.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich466\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6990
